AD  - ESME Research Lab, Ivry sur Seine, France
AD  - Department of Apprenticeship Embedded Systems and Intelligent Transportation Systems, ESME, France
AB  - Head-Up Displays (HUDs) are mission-critical cockpit interfaces in fighter aircraft, requiring high visual stability under extreme flight dynamics including rapid rotations, structural vibrations, and aggressive maneuvering. Conventional inertial stabilization approaches rely on fixed model-based filtering techniques that lack adaptability to rapidly varying motion conditions, often resulting in residual visual jitter and increased pilot cognitive workload. This paper proposes a deterministic embedded machine learning approach for adaptive fighter aircraft HUD stabilization. The core contribution lies in integrating lightweight on-device intelligence capable of real-time inertial motion classification under strict memory and latency constraints compatible with safety-critical avionics systems. Using tri-axial gyroscope data, the system classifies four fundamental aircraft motion states-yaw, roll, pitch, and level flight-through temporal signal windows of 1536 samples (512 per axis). A comparative benchmarking study evaluates Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP), Random Forest (RF), Support Vector Machine (SVM), SEFR, and Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) models trained on 150 motion sequences per class. Experimental results demonstrate that the MLP-based implementation achieves the optimal trade-off between classification accuracy and embedded resource usage, reaching a balanced accuracy of 97.50% and a quality index of 93.5, while requiring only 5.8 KB RAM, 24 KB Flash memory, and 3.3 ms Worst Case Execution Time (WCET) on a microcontroller-class platform. The deterministic execution behavior ensures bounded latency suitable for real-time avionics applications. Compared to conventional inertial stabilization methods, the proposed adaptive strategy enables context-aware HUD rendering with improved visual robustness and operational reliability under dynamic flight conditions. © 2026 IEEE.
