AB  - Digital Signal Processing (DSP) constitutes a fundamental component of contemporary electronic engineering. Nevertheless, conventional pedagogical approaches to the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) often create a divide between theoretical derivation and practical engineering application. Typical curricula emphasize the asymptotic time complexity of O(Nlog2N) while frequently overlooking the critical limitations imposed by resource-constrained embedded platforms. To address this discrepancy, this paper proposes a pedagogical framework entitled Theoretical Tracing–Engineering Reconstruction–Hardware Implementation. Employing the Raspberry Pi Pico 2 W (ARM Cortex-M33) as the primary experimental platform, an ablation-incremental evaluation matrix was developed to systematically exploit MicroPython’s Native and Viper code emitters. The findings indicate that an integrated hardware-software co-design approach yields a substantial improvement in performance, achieving an overall execution speedup of 61.2 × . Importantly, the research uncovers a counterintuitive architectural insight: memory bandwidth, rather than arithmetic logic unit (ALU) throughput, represents the principal bottleneck in modern pipelined microcontrollers, as demonstrated by the inability of arithmetically optimized algorithms to achieve speed enhancements. This multi-level instructional methodology redefines the engineering understanding of computational complexity and markedly improves students’ practical skills within the domain of contemporary embedded systems. © 2026 The Author(s).
