AD  - Tezpur University, Department Of Computer Science And Engineering, Assam, Tezpur, India
AB  - With the rapid proliferation of increasingly sophisticated malware, there is a critical need for robust and efficient detection methods capable of identifying both obfuscated and non-obfuscated threats. Image-based malware classification using deep learning offers significant advantages by enabling the automatic learning of complex patterns, thereby eliminating the need for manual feature engineering. In this work, two deep learning frameworks were designed to accurately classify obfuscated and non-obfuscated samples of both benign and malicious Android applications. The publicly available AndroDex dataset is utilized for experimental validation. The proposed approaches are evaluated and compared against state-of-the-art transfer learning models, including EfficientNetB7 and MobileNetV3Large, in terms of classification performance and model complexity. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed binarized neural network achieves competitive performance while significantly reducing parameter size. Specifically, the model attains average 10-fold cross-validation scores of 94.27% accuracy, 95% precision, 94.23% recall, and 94.27% F1-score. These findings highlight the potential of the proposed approach for effective and lightweight malware detection, particularly in resource-constrained hardware such as microcontrollers, FPGAs, etc., because the network has very low memory footprints. Moreover, the deployment of the proposed framework on hardware devices enabling near real-time detection may be considered an intriguing possibility for future research. © 2026 IEEE.
