AD  - California State University, Northridge, Los Angeles, CA, United States
AD  - California State University, Northridge, Northridge, CA, United States
AB  - Wearable health monitoring must operate under real-world motion artifacts, missing sensors, and strict constraints on latency, memory, and connectivity. This paper presents an end-to-end edge intelligence system for multimodal biomedical monitoring that fuses electrocardiography (ECG), photoplethysmography (PPG), inertial measurement unit (IMU) motion, and electrodermal activity (EDA) into a unified window representation, producing context-aware alerts. A compact multi-task neural model jointly predicts activity (8 classes), stress (4 classes), and arrhythmia (binary) from 10-second windows sampled at 100 Hz. In 5-fold cross-validation, the system achieves 52.3%±1.8% activity accuracy, 69.4%±4.2% stress accuracy, and 75.0%±6.6% arrhythmia accuracy. A deterministic motion gate derived from accelerometer statistics prevents cascading alert failures under activity misclassification.We demonstrate embedded deployment on an ESP32-S3-class SoC with a reduced 6-channel sensor subset and a 15.8Kparameter CNN (∼61.6 KB FP32). For arrhythmia detection, the system achieves an AUC-ROC of 0.843±0.077, with a sensitivity of 70.3% and a specificity of 80.1%, suitable for screening applications. Evaluation on a constructed four-case alert benchmark validates motion-aware alerting, confirming the system's ability to provide reliable safety-critical monitoring on resource-constrained hardware. © 2026 IEEE.
