AD  - Krishna Institute of Engineering and Technology (KIET), Department of ECE, UP, Ghaziabad, India
AB  - Nanosatellite payload operations face severe constraints in power, communication bandwidth, onboard memory, and ground-station visibility windows, which together make highvolume downlink difficult. This work presents Payload Vision, an end-to-end, ground-validated prototype that demonstrates both an efficient telemetry communication pipe and a low-rate image-transfer workflow using low-cost Commercial-Off-TheShelf (COTS) hardware. The telemetry subsystem uses lossless compression, packetization, and Stop-and-Wait Automatic Repeat Request (ARQ) to support reliable delivery with reduced airtime. For the imaging pipeline, a Raspberry Pi 4 captures a scene using a Pi camera, preprocesses the image through resizing and quality reduction, and transmits it using a LoRa Transmitter interfaced with a micro-controller in fixed-size chunks to a LoRa receiver interfaced with a micro-controller, which reconstructs the file on an SD card. After image reconstruction, the received image is passed through a trained debris-classification model to identify whether the object belongs to a targeted space-debris class, providing a post-downlink intelligence layer. This combined workflow demonstrates a reproducible approach for robust telemetry, low-rate image transfer, and lightweight onboard-orground image interpretation under nanosatellite-like constraints. © 2026 IEEE.
