AD  - Concordia University, Department of Computer Science and Software Engineering, Montreal, H3G 1M8, QC, Canada
AB  - Low-power wide area network (LPWAN) technologies have accelerated the growth of the Internet of Things (IoT) by enabling low-power operation and wide area coverage for constrained devices. However, limitations such as small maximum transfer unit (MTU) sizes and a lack of fragmentation support restrict the use of standard IP-based protocols. The static context header compression and fragmentation (SCHC) standard addresses these constraints, allowing the transmission of large packets, including IPv6, over LPWANs. Nonetheless, SCHC faces performance challenges in networks with long delays and intermittent connectivity, such as Direct-to-Satellite IoT (DtS-IoT), where devices connect directly to sparse low Earth orbit (LEO) satellite constellations. This work analyzes SCHC confirmed delivery modes under delay- and disruption-prone conditions. We introduce a model that estimates the total packet transfer delay and the energy consumption of three SCHC acknowledgment mechanisms used in reliable fragmentation, including the recently added Compound ACK procedure. The model is validated in a LoRaWAN-based DtS-IoT scenario that integrates satellite visibility forecasts for the Swarm constellation, SCHC profile parameters for LoRaWAN (RFC 9011), and realistic link characteristics such as frame loss rate (FLR) and time-on-air for LoRa and LR-FHSS modulations. Our results identify the acknowledgment mechanism with the shortest transmission time and quantify its impact on energy usage. The model and validation approach support the choice of the most energy-efficient SCHC fragmentation mode for constrained IoT devices operating under intermittent connectivity, helping advance sustainable DtS-IoT deployments.  © 2026 IEEE.
