AD  - Xiangtan University, School of Physics and Optoelectronics, Xiangtan, China
AD  - Peking University, School of Electronics Engineering, Beijing, China
AB  - Deploying Spiking Neural Networks (SNNs) on edge microcontrollers (MCUs) offers a highly promising pathway for ultra-low-power artificial intelligence. However, mapping highly biomimetic SNNs onto standard von Neumann architectures introduces severe memory walls and computing mismatches. Specifically, the conventional spatial unrolling of time steps induces an unsustainable expansion of dynamic activation buffers, frequently triggering Out-of-Memory (OOM) failures on resource-constrained devices. To bridge this gap, we propose a hardware-software co-optimization architecture tailored for ARM Cortex-M7 processors. First, we introduce a double-depth rolling array mechanism that deeply decouples sequence length from memory footprint, reducing the spatial complexity of dynamic hidden states from O(N×T) to a constant O(N). Second, an event-driven sparse execution restructuring based on the Coordinate List (COO) format is implemented to bypass resting neurons, transforming power-intensive dense Multiply-Accumulate (MAC) operations into single-cycle conditional additions. Supported by an asymmetric mixed-precision mapping strategy, our architecture strictly preserves complex neural dynamics. Crucially, rigorous white-box testing confirms a bounded state error of 2.38×10-6 with zero spike-timing deviation, guaranteeing a mathematically lossless accuracy migration. Physical benchmarks on the STM32H7 MCU across both static MNIST and continuous Speech wake-word tasks demonstrate that the proposed framework slashes peak dynamic SRAM overhead by nearly 74%, completely eliminating OOM vulnerabilities. Evaluated on the MNIST task using a recurrent SNN (784-256-10) with partial readout, the system achieves an inference latency of 197.69 ms and an energy consumption of 76.98 mJ, significantly outperforming iso-parametric recurrent neural networks in both temporal and spatial dimensions. © 2026 IEEE.
