AD  - National University of Singapore, Singapore
AD  - Birla Institute of Technology and Science (BITS) Pilani, Rajasthan, Pilani, India
AD  - Birla Institute of Technology and Science (BITS) Pilani, Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Rajasthan, Pilani, India
AB  - Tiny Machine Learning (TinyML) is an innovative technology paradigm that allows Machine Learning (ML) inference on resource-constrained microcontrollers. This paper surveys TinyML for transportation systems. Eight application areas from the literature are covered: (i) intrusion detection for in-vehicle networks and EV-charging interfaces, (ii) safety monitoring (alcohol impairment, drowsiness, falls), (iii) vehicle perception in embedded vision, (iv) environmental impact estimation (fuel type classification, CO2 soft sensors), (v) vehicle diagnostics for automotive and rail systems, (vi) driver behavior analytics, (vii) automotive enhancements, and (viii) traffic control with adaptive signal timing. We highlight representative deployments on MCUs (Microcontroller Units) across these domains and present their quantization choices, memory footprints, and real-time performance. Furthermore, we present the limitations of TinyML, including hardware resource constraints, framework fragmentation, data scarcity, robustness–accuracy trade-offs, integration challenges, constraints on online learning, and application-specific challenges. TinyML is a promising enabler of responsive, private, and affordable technology for transportation intelligence, but progress requires standardized toolchains and benchmarks, richer open datasets, robustness methods for non-stationary environments, and lightweight on-device adaptation. © 2012 IEEE.
