TI  - RA-LaWKeR: RAM-Aware Kernels Reconstruction With Tile-Based LRA for Efficient Deep Learning on MCUs
PY  - 2026
T2  - IEEE Internet of Things Journal
VL  - 13
IS  - 16
SP  - 37565
EP  - 37574
DO  - 10.1109/JIOT.2026.3701635
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041960358?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Electrical Engineering, Indian Institute of Technology Patna, Patna, 801106, India
AB  - As edge devices become increasingly affordable and capable, artificial intelligence (AI) is being applied to problems ranging from everyday applications to highly complex tasks. This trend has driven a growing interest in integrating AI models directly on resource-constrained microcontrollers (MCUs), enabling low-latency, energy-efficient, and privacy-preserving on-device intelligence. However, deploying deep learning (DL) models on MCUs remains highly challenging due to severe limitations in on-chip memory, restricted static random access memory (SRAM) availability during runtime, and the need to accommodate stack, heap, and interrupt-driven memory usage. These constraints often lead to inference failures or make deployment infeasible for larger models. To address these challenges, this work presents an enhanced compression and reconstruction strategy that combines tile-based low-rank approximation (LRA) with a novel RAM-aware layerwise kernel reconstruction (RA-LaWKeR) method, enabling efficient on-chip memory utilization and reliable inference on memory-constrained MCUs. Unlike our earlier LaWKeR approach, which relied on fixed RAM allocation and single-kernel reconstruction, the proposed RA-LaWKeR framework dynamically monitors available SRAM during runtime and supports multikernel reconstruction. This adaptive strategy significantly reduces frequent Flash memory accesses by retaining a subset of reconstructed kernels in heap memory, thereby minimizing repeated reconstruction overhead. As a result, the proposed method improves inference latency and enables RAM-adaptive execution on MCUs. Furthermore, on devices with larger available SRAM, RA-LaWKeR automatically exploits the additional memory to retain more kernels, leading to improved runtime efficiency without requiring any architectural changes. We validate the proposed method on the STM32H743 MCU, where the model executes in only 183 ms while efficiently managing both Flash and SRAM usage. Compared to existing tinyML frameworks and hardware-aware compression toolkits, RA-LaWKeR achieves superior memory optimization by adaptively balancing Flash compression with SRAM reconstruction, making it a promising solution for deploying DL models on edge MCUs. © 2026 IEEE. All rights reserved.
