AB  - With the rapid development of the Internet of Things (IoT) and edge computing, deploying deep learning models on resource-constrained microcontrollers (MCUs) has become a significant challenge due to limited computational power, memory capacity, and energy budget. DenseNet, an efficient deep learning architecture known for its parameter efficiency and feature reuse, is widely used in tasks such as image classification and object detection. However, its high computational and memory demands make it difficult to deploy on embedded devices. This paper presents a method for optimizing DenseNet deployment on MCUs through dynamic memory management. The approach adjusts memory allocation strategies during the inference process, improving memory usage efficiency and reducing energy consumption without compromising model performance. Experimental results demonstrate that the optimized DenseNet model (98K parameters) consumes only 32KB of SRAM per inference and achieves a 97.6% accuracy on the MNIST dataset after quantization, with an inference time of 550us. Furthermore, an optimized CNN model (24K parameters) achieves 96.1% accuracy, using 19KB of SRAM per inference. These results highlight the effectiveness of the proposed method in deploying deep learning models on resource-constrained embedded systems. © 2025 SPIE.
