AD  - Department of Computer Science, Multidisciplinary Faculty of Nador, University Mohammed Premier, Oujda, Morocco
AD  - Laboratory LaSTI, Department of Networks, Telecommunications and Cybersecurity, ENSA Khouribga, Sultan Moulay Slimane University, Beni Mellal, Morocco
AB  - Mask detection has become a critical tool for promoting safety and regulatory compliance in both public and private environments. This study presents a comprehensive Tiny Machine Learning (TinyML) application that utilizes Vision Wake Words (VWW) to facilitate real-time monitoring of mask usage. The system is built on the lightweight MobileNetV2 architecture, which uses depthwise separable convolutions to reduce memory usage and computational overhead, making it suitable for deployment on microcontroller units (MCUs). To streamline the development process, transfer learning is applied, allowing for rapid adaptation to the mask detection task while minimizing the need for extensive training data and time. Sensor inputs and preprocessing steps are optimized to handle the large data volumes typical in machine learning pipelines. The initial model size of 8.62 MB was successfully reduced to just 214 KB using TensorFlow Lite’s dynamic range quantization, enabling efficient deployment on highly resource-constrained devices. Achieving a detection accuracy of 99.6%, the proposed model demonstrates the effectiveness of TinyML in real-world safety applications. Supporting the goals of Healthcare 4.0, this approach integrates smart sensing and IoT technologies, highlighting the potential of TinyML to deliver scalable, cost-effective public health solutions with minimal hardware requirements. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Khalid El Makkaoui, Ismail Lamaakal, Ibrahim Ouahbi, Yassine Maleh, and Ahmed A. Abd El-Latif; individual chapters, the contributors.
