TI  - Adaptive Scale Networks: Stable Self-Optimizing AI Models
PY  - 2026
T2  - Procedia Computer Science
VL  - 282
SP  - 2894
EP  - 2909
DO  - 10.1016/j.procs.2026.06.057
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045569965?origin=resultslist
AD  - Computer Science & Engineering Department, Shoolini University, Solan, 173234, India
AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) are typically deployed as static architectures, limiting their efficiency and adaptability. While dynamic methods like Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) and test-time adaptation promise task-specific gains, they often suffer from oscillatory allocation, gradient explosions, and compute budget violations unsuitable for production environments. This paper introduces Adaptive Scale Networks (ASN), an inference-time self-specialization framework achieving zero budget violations (0/15,000 steps) and perfect gradient finiteness (15,000/15,000 steps) across optimization on an NVIDIA T4 GPU. ASN addresses three coupled failure modes through: (1) variance-adaptive smoothing of attention entropy statistics; (2) temperature-controlled soft discretization with δ-threshold hysteresis preventing rank thrashing; and (3) spectrally-capped mixture-of-scales (MOS) SVF adapters ensuring bounded operator norms (∥∆∥2 ≤ σmax = 2.0). Implemented on GPT-2 (124M parameters) with 1.2M trainable adapter parameters (1.0% overhead), ASN demonstrates stability with dynamic rank allocation from 4 to 16 per layer under strict budget constraints (B = 144). Training achieves 0.087% evaluation loss reduction (10.404 → 10.395) while maintaining perfect budget adherence. The architecture learns adaptive scale selection, with π(8) converging to 0.71. ASN eliminates gradient instability (0% NaN/Inf vs. typical 5-15% in dynamic methods) and achieves 100% budget compliance (vs. 85-95% in soft-constrained systems), maintaining efficiency comparable to static LoRA. Experimental findings demonstrate that ASN delivers reliable, deterministic, and adaptive computation without sacrificing stability or performance. These results indicate that ASN offers a practical path towards deploying adaptive LLMs safety in real-world, resource-constrained environments. © 2026 The Authors.
