AD  - University of Texas at Dallas, Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Richardson, United States
AB  - Continuous EEG monitoring is critical for detecting neurological abnormalities, yet many automated EEG analysis methods rely on computationally intensive models that are impractical for wearable and embedded devices. This paper presents a lightweight TinyML-based framework for real-time EEG abnormality classification on resource-constrained hardware. Using an open, expert-annotated scalp EEG dataset, EEG recordings are segmented and transformed into compact spectral and statistical feature representations suitable for microcontroller-class deployment. To reduce sensing and computational overhead, the proposed approach operates on a limited subset of EEG channels from frontal, central, and parietal regions. Two neural network models, a fully connected deep neural network (DNN) and a onedimensional convolutional neural network (1D CNN), are trained and optimized for ESP32-class microcontrollers. Experimental results show that the compact DNN achieves higher classification accuracy with lower latency and memory usage than the 1D CNN under strict deployment constraints. These findings demonstrate that accurate EEG-based normal and abnormality (pathological patterns) classification can be performed entirely on-device using TinyML, enabling practical real-time EEG monitoring without cloud dependency. © 2026 IEEE.
