AD  - School of Computer Science and Technology, Zhejiang Gongshang University, Hangzhou, 310018, China
AD  - State Key Laboratory of Industrial Control Technology, Institute of Cyber Systems and Control, Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, 310027, China
AD  - School of Environmental Science and Engineering, Zhejiang Gongshang University, Hangzhou, 310018, China
AB  - Leveraging convolutional neural networks (CNNs) to process high-dimensional time-series data in electronic noses (e-noses) holds significant promise. However, several challenges persist, including odor pattern representation and model optimization for embedded devices. Here, we propose a lightweight odor recognition method called OdorNet, designed for TinyML implementation in handheld e-noses powered by resource-constrained microcontrollers. OdorNet consists of Absolutized Difference Sensor Response (ADSR), Markov Transition Field (MTF), and a simplified convolutional encoder network (LiteCEN). The core concept is to convert the one-dimensional sensor response sequence at a given time into a two-dimensional pseudo-image using the MTF, which is then processed by LiteCEN for odor recognition. Particularly, these images are further expanded in both spatial and temporal dimensions through the ADSR and sparse frame concatenation, allowing for extraction of more information from fewer sensors. The proposed method achieves an average recognition accuracy of 99.42 % in classifying six types of vinegar, outperforming LeNet-5 with the original MTF images and other methods. Furthermore, we find that its performance remains stable with reduced sampling durations, even when only the gas intake phase is retained, significantly shortening the time required from detection to decision-making. Finally, OdorNet is seamlessly integrated into a handheld e-nose with the ESP32-S3 microcontroller for real-time, end-to-end odor recognition, owing to its low computational complexity and compact memory footprint. Moreover, OdorNet is effectively validated on a public dataset and compared with other CNN architectures. The results demonstrate that OdorNet is a promising method for edge computing, offering significant advantages in cost-efficiency and miniaturization for e-noses. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
