TI  - Why QLoRa Fails on CPUs: A Comparative Evaluation with LoRA for Efficient Fine-Tuning of LLMS on Resource-Constrained Environments
PY  - 2026
T2  - CCIC 2026 - Contemporary Computing Innovations Conference 2026
DO  - 10.1109/CCIC68129.2026.11486146
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105038752047?origin=resultslist
AD  - Alliance College of Engineering and Design, Alliance University, Bangalore, India
AB  - When memory is limited and full parameter updates are not possible, QLoRA is frequently cited as an effective method for fine-tuning big language models. GPU configurations account for almost all of the results that have been published to support its performance. It's considerably less clear what occurs when you try running it on CPUs alone. This work uses practical experiments to examine that particular case. Dependency parsing was our test workload, and we ran everything on standard CPU hardware, including local ARM desktops and cloud x86 instances, with no GPU involvement. Rather than focusing on quick training or low cost, the key concern was whether the strategy could be used consistently. The answer turned out to be no in most cases. QLoRA repeatedly failed to get past initialization, or it would freeze during forward passes, or just quit early. These problems showed up consistently, regardless of model size or machine type. Meanwhile, plain LoRA worked smoothly under the same conditions and gave repeatable results. Such problems appear to be related to internal assumptions in QLoRA design and quantization implementation, but not to data-related tasks such as parsing. In any case, here is a bottom line: it is high time to abandon any assumption that quantization-aware models such as QLoRA will behave in a hardware-agnostic fashion. The only thing that matters is hardware-specific tests.  © 2026 IEEE.
