AB  - The Robot Operating System 2 (ROS 2) ecosystem and its embedded variant micro-ROS are increasingly used in Cyber-Physical Systems (CPS) on Microcontroller Units (MCUs). micro-ROS clients communicate through the Data Distribution Service (DDS) profile for eXtremely Resource-Constrained Environments (XRCE-DDS), but this middleware has no built-in way to verify client firmware integrity before nodes join the network, so a compromised client can join and disseminate malicious data. Existing mechanisms such as secure boot and Trusted Platform Module (TPM)-based remote attestation let verifiers check that a device boots an expected image, but they usually require dedicated TPM hardware and are ill-suited to low-end MCU-based platforms. MCU attestation schemes have been proposed, but they largely remain research prototypes and are not integrated with ROS 2 or XRCE-DDS, so micro-ROS lacks a standard way to enforce firmware integrity at network admission time. In this paper, we propose SERA, a secure mechanism that embeds remote attestation into the Micro XRCE-DDS handshake, requiring each client to prove its firmware integrity before joining the DDS network. SERA leverages Roots of Trust, including the Device Identifier Composition Engine (DICE), Arm Platform Security Architecture (Arm PSA), the Memory Protection Unit (MPU), and Arm TrustZone-M to offer isolation profiles on MCUs. On a NUCLEO-L552ZE-Q board, SERA adds handshake latency of 380–795 ms and increases initialization time and firmware size compared to a baseline without attestation, while keeping startup overheads practical. In targeted experiments, SERA successfully prevents firmware tampering and replay attacks from joining the DDS network. © 2013 IEEE.
