AD  - Centreon Corp, Santa Clara, United States
AD  - Arete Microelectronics, Hsinchu, Taiwan
AB  - Machine learning inference is increasingly being deployed on ultra-low-power microcontrollers and edge devices, enabling intelligent control in resource-constrained environments. Such systems must operate under stringent limits on power consumption, memory capacity, and the cost, motivating the development of highly efficient compute architecture. This paper presents a high-performance RISC-V based microcontroller embedded with a hardware-accelerated Mathematical Function Unit (MFU), and a compute-in-memory (CIM) accelerator optimized for AI and HPC. The proposed architecture efficiently supports transcendental and OpenCL math functions with full IEEE-754 denormal handling and exact rounding and a tightly-coupled input buffer and MAC compute accelerator. Compared with conventional software implementations, the AI-optimized MFU delivers up to two orders of magnitude speedup over software implementations while requiring only 0.5x the area of fused multiply-accumulate (FMA) per function. Fabricated in 28-nm CMOS technology, the prototype operates at 1-GHz and achieves peak throughput of up to 744 GFLOPS FP16 at less than 2W. Measurement results show that transcendental functions complete within 10 cycles, enabling real-time edge inference while preserving numerical accuracy critical for AI training convergence, operating at 0.81 V and 0°C. Notably, the MFU incurs only a small hardware gate overhead, contributes less than 0.2% of total power consumption, and improves overall system performance by 20∼40%, demonstrating high energy efficiency with minimal area and power penalty. © 2026 IEEE.
