AD  - Department of Computer Science and Engineering, NMAMIT, Nitte (Deemed to be University), Karnataka, India
AD  - Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Sahyadri College of Engineering and Management, Mangalore, India
AB  - Resource constraints on devices, to some extent, and the increasing threat posed by quantum computing both present tremendous challenges in improving effective security protocols for the Internet of Things (IoT). Herein we present the Quantum-Threat Lightweight Cipher (QTL-Cipher), a novel hybrid cryptographic algorithm for an Internet of Things ecosystem, in response to this challenge. The design was based on employing an asymmetric, post-quantum key generation mechanism and a lightweight symmetric module for confidentiality. The key exchange process utilized a unique, lattice-based method based on the Ring-Learning with Rounding problem, while the core symmetric module utilized a custom hardware efficient Feistel network structure. An important part of our approach involves a resource-aware scheduling paradigm that adjusts cryptographic parameters, for example Feistel rounds and S-box permutations, on-the-fly, based on device state parameters, for example battery level and processing load, to ensure the best possible device resource utilization without compromising security of any kind. Design and testing were conducted using a common IoT hardware platform, the ARM Cortex-M series microcontroller. Performance results indicated that our approach reduced energy consumption by 40% and RAM usage by 35% as compared to AES-256, Rigorous security analysis confirms the algorithm's resilience against both classical and quantum attacks. This work concludes that the proposed QTLCipher design presents a robust, efficient, and quantum-resistant solution for ensuring data confidentiality in resource-constrained IoT applications. © 2026, Nexora Academic Press. All rights reserved.
