AD  - College of Engineering Technology for Computers and Artificial Intelligence, Northern Technical University, Kirkuk, Iraq
AB  - Federated intrusion detection systems (IDS) for Internet of Vehicles (IoV) CAN-bus networks are typically evaluated on detection accuracy and adversarial robustness alone, leaving their practical deployability on resource-constrained Electronic Control Units (ECUs) unexamined. This paper extends the CNN-BiGRU-Attention federated IDS architecture and the adversarially robust Adaptive Weighted Input (AWI) aggregation mechanism established in prior work [1]–[3] with a dedicated, layer-wise computational complexity and edge-feasibility analysis. Using standard parameter-count and multiply-accumulate (MAC) formulas applied directly to the architecture specification validated in [1] and [3], we derive new layer-by-layer parameter and computational-cost breakdowns, quantify the memory footprint of the full model and of each architectural branch under FP32, FP16, and INT8 precision, and estimate inference latency on representative automotive-grade microcontroller platforms. The full architecture totals 116,097 trainable parameters and approximately 0.93 million floating-point operations (MFLOPs) per inference, occupying 453.5 KB at FP32 precision and 113.4 KB under INT8 quantization — well within the SRAM budget of common automotive-grade microcontrollers. Cross-referencing these computed costs against the accuracy figures independently reported in [1] and [3] shows that the convolutional branch delivers the highest accuracy-per-parameter efficiency of the three architectural components, and that the dual-stage adversarial defense mechanism improves robustness without adding any inference-time parameters, since it operates exclusively at the server-side aggregation stage. These findings corroborate and extend the design choices validated in [1]–[3], providing the deployment-oriented evidence needed to support real-vehicle ECU integration of the proposed framework.
