AB  - In resource-constrained economies across Asia and Africa, where approximately 700 million individuals subsist below the international poverty threshold of 2.15 per day (World Bank, 2025), government-sponsored Public Distribution Systems (PDS) designed to provide subsidized rations to underprivileged populations are frequently challenged by inefficiencies and irregularities associated with antiquated manual and paper-based authentication protocols. Such limitations may enable data inconsistencies, inaccurate entitlement tracking, and administrative leakages, thereby affecting transparency and operational efficiency. To address these challenges, this manuscript presents a WEB server-integrated IoT-enabled RFID ATM ration vending prototype intended to demonstrate a technological approach for improving transparency and automation in localized PDS environments. The proposed architecture employs an Xtensa LX7 dual-core microcontroller coordinating RFID readers, relay modules, and feedback mechanisms, combined with dual dispensing mechanisms: a precision spring-actuated dispenser for pre-packaged rations ranging from 0.5 to 5 kg, and a solenoid-controlled hopper mechanism for volumetric grain dispensing between 1 and 5 kg. Beneficiary RFID credentials interface with an SQL and Python backend through a Tkinter-based graphical user interface (GUI), enabling wireless command transmission via a dedicated web server for quota management and transaction recording. A prototype implementation of the system was developed and tested in a controlled environment to validate the operational workflow and feasibility of automated ration dispensing. Experimental observations indicate approximately 98% dispensing fidelity, while the automated framework demonstrates the potential to reduce manual administrative involvement in ration distribution tasks. The presented system therefore serves as a prototype-level technological model illustrating how embedded automation and digital record management may contribute toward improving transparency and efficiency in welfare-oriented distribution systems. © 2026 IEEE.
