AD  - London Metropolitan University, School of Computing and Digital Media, London, United Kingdom
AB  - Blockchain systems are typically associated with distributed consensus and resource-intensive peer-to-peer networks, making them unsuitable for deployment on microcontroller-class devices. However, many embedded Internet of Things and edge artificial intelligence systems require tamper-evident logging mechanisms without relying on continuous cloud connectivity or distributed infrastructure. This paper presents a lightweight on-device blockchain framework designed specifically for resource-constrained microcontrollers. The proposed framework uses core blockchain principles, including the use of hash-linked blocks and append-only storage, while eliminating distributed consensus, mining, and peer-to-peer synchronization. The framework is implemented in C/C++ and deployed on ESP32-class hardware, using fixed-size block structures, deterministic memory usage, SHA-256 hashing and flash-based persistent storage. Experimental evaluation shows that the framework achieves transaction append times of 32-74 ms, block creation and SHA-256 commit times of 41-86 ms, and chain verification times of 1-4 ms for chains of up to ten blocks. Persistent storage grew linearly from 280 bytes after genesis creation to 3597 bytes after ten blocks, corresponding to approximately 330-340 bytes per block. Runtime heap remained stable at approximately 304 KB. These results demonstrate that blockchain-inspired integrity mechanisms can be adapted effectively to microcontroller environments for trustworthy local event logging without distributed consensus or external infrastructure.  © 2026 IEEE.
