AB  - Emotion-aware speech synthesis on edge devices is gaining traction in applications such as conversational agents, healthcare monitoring, and assistive robotics. However, existing GAN-based emotional speech models are typically static, limiting their responsiveness to new speakers, accents, environments, and personalised affective expression. This study introduces a Dynamic Model Adaptation Framework that enables on-device and hybrid on-device/offloaded continual adaptation using lightweight adapter modules such as LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) and convolutional bottleneck adapters. Instead of retraining the full Emotion Speaker GAN, the proposed method updates only 1-5% of the parameters, allowing real-time personalization while maintaining low computational overhead. This work evaluates the framework across CPUs, GPUs, and Jetson Nano devices. Experiments show that the proposed adaptive system preserves up to 95% of original MOS quality, with an average adaptation time of 12-25 seconds for 10-15 gradient steps. Memory overhead remains below 20 MB, and latency increases by less than 8%, making the system suitable for continuous personalization in resource-constrained environments. This work establishes a practical pathway to deploy continually improving, privacypreserving, and user-adaptive emotional speech GANs on edge hardware. © 2026 IEEE.
