AB  - Deep learning (DL) is gradually becoming the most widely used computational approach in the field of mechanical fault diagnosis. However, the restricted hardware resources of mechanical vibration wireless sensor networks (WSNs) pose a challenge for DL deployment. In this article, we propose a convolutional neural networks (CNNs) hardware accelerator to address this challenge. First, a strategy of operator-sharing processing element (PE) arrays is employed to reduce the consumption of hardware logic resources. Various floating-point operation modes are achieved by configuring the input port data of the PE unit. Second, a memory-mapping controller (MMC) is used to control the address mapping and data interactions between PE arrays, operators, and memories based on the index of the model. Finally, the accelerator employs a definable number of PE units and clock frequencies to make decisions based on available hardware logic resources. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first instance of deploying a hardware accelerator for CNNs on a mechanical vibration WSNs node. Meanwhile, we validate the CNN model using the gearbox dataset from the Drivetrain Diagnostics Simulator (DDS), and develop a WSNs node equipped with a field programmable gate array (FPGA) to validate the accelerator's performance. The experimental results demonstrate that, compared to the high-performance microcontroller at the same power consumption level, our deployment solution achieves a 10-fold improvement in inference speed while maintaining the accuracy of fault diagnosis.  © 2026 IEEE.
