TI  - Deep Learning-based Wearable IoT Health Monitoring System for Real-Time ECG and SpO2 Abnormality Detection
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of 7th International Conference on Smart Systems and Inventive Technology, ICSSIT 2026
SP  - 1
EP  - 6
DO  - 10.1109/ICSSIT69151.2026.11656576
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049065084?origin=resultslist
AD  - Vignan's Foundation for Science, Technology and Research, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Guntur, India
AB  - Cardiovascular diseases remain the single largest contributor to global mortality, accounting for nearly a third of all deaths recorded worldwide each year. Continuous, unobtrusive monitoring of cardiac activity is therefore a pressing clinical need, and wearable Internet-of-Things (IoT) platforms offer a compelling pathway to meet it. This paper introduces a comprehensive deep learning framework that performs realtime abnormality detection on two complementary physiological streams - electrocardiogram (ECG) and blood oxygen saturation (SpO2) - acquired from body-worn sensors. Four neural architectures are designed, trained, and systematically compared: a CNN-LSTM baseline, a novel Attention-augmented Bidirectional LSTM (Attention-BiLSTM), a multimodal ECG-SpO2 fusion network, and an edge-optimised lightweight model intended for microcontroller deployment. ECG data are drawn from the publicly available MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database, while SpO2 sequences are produced by a physiologically-informed signal simulator. Among all evaluated architectures, the Attention-BiLSTM achieves the highest classification accuracy of 96.7% and an AUC-ROC of 0.991 at an inference latency of just 12.4ms, making it well-suited for real-time wearable applications. The edge-optimised variant reaches a footprint below 50KB under float16 quantisation, enabling deployment on resource-constrained ARM Cortex-M4 processors. A Flask-based IoT dashboard with MQTT integration provides live ECG visualisation and clinical alert generation. The results confirm that the proposed system delivers clinical-grade accuracy while remaining computationally efficient enough for battery-powered wearable devices. © 2026 IEEE.
