AB  - High-precision question answering (QA) is a critical requirement for biomedical large language models (LLMs). However, the prohibitive computational cost of training such models from scratch forces many researchers to adapt existing open-source biomedical LLMs based on the Transformer architecture. Although recent models such as BioMistral-7B and MedAlpaca-7B achieve a favorable balance between parameter efficiency and accuracy, they are primarily optimized for general biomedical coverage rather than specialized subdomains. Motivated by this limitation and the need for efficient domain adaptation under constrained computational resources, we propose Sequential Layer-Selective Rank Reduction (SLSR), an enhanced variant of the LASER approach. While building upon the LASER framework, SLSR introduces domain-specific innovations including a sequential combinatorial search strategy and greedy accuracy-threshold-based layer retention mechanism tailored for biomedical LLM and VLM optimization. SLSR iteratively applies layer-selective rank reduction to a single type of linear layer from shallow to deep layers without retraining, guided by threshold-constrained combinatorial optimization. Experimental results on ten specific subdomains of the public biomedical QA datasets across four LLMs demonstrate an average relative accuracy improvement of 6.12%, with particularly strong gains in domain-specific tasks. Furthermore, SLSR is readily extensible to vision-language models (VLMs), achieving a mean relative accuracy improvement of 10.57% on five biomedical visual QA benchmarks. As a complement to the time consumption metric (in hours) for evaluating computational efficiency, we introduce the Memory-Time Product (MTP) metric, which incorporates GPU memory usage (in GB) alongside time consumption to provide a more comprehensive assessment of computational efficiency during model optimization. Considering both metrics together, SLSR demonstrates superior overall computational efficiency compared to LASER and low-rank adaptation (LoRA). These results provide practical guidance for efficient adaptation of biomedical LLMs and VLMs in resource-constrained settings. © 2017 IEEE.
