AB  - Tiny machine learning (TinyML) refers to a new paradigm enabling machine learning (ML) on resource-constrained, low-power devices, which revolutionized the Internet of Things (IoT) by bringing intelligence to the edge. This chapter provides in-depth capabilities, limitations, and applications on TinyML devices and tools. This chapter further explores some hardware platforms, such as microcontrollers and single-board computers, that further state their ability to perform real-time and low-power operations. It also covers essential software frameworks like TensorFlow Lite (TFL) and Edge Impulse, which are applied to deploying ML models in these constrained devices. This chapter introduces advanced techniques in model quantization, pruning, and neural architecture search (NAS). It shows their effectiveness in obtaining highly optimized model sizes with energy efficiency without losing as much accuracy as other lightweight models. In this direction, it also discusses the critical trade-off needed in these advances between computational complexity, memory constraints, and inference latency. It introduces equations and figures to explain key concepts. Finally, the experience of existing deployments indicates the more significant importance of power efficiency, data privacy, and real-time decision-making in TinyML systems. Specifically, future perspectives highlight mature hardware accelerators, unified frameworks, and standardized benchmarks toward enhancing impact in areas such as healthcare, smart agriculture, and industrial IoT by TinyML. Now, scalable, cost-effective, and privacy-preserving TinyML solutions for next-generation IoT applications are built. This chapter contains a guide for researchers, developers, and practitioners using TinyML to build innovative and impactful real-world applications. © 2026 by The Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers, Inc. All rights reserved.
