AD  - Department of Computer Science and Automatic Control, School of Computing Engineering, Universidad Nacional de Educación a Distancia (UNED), Madrid, 28040, Spain
AB  - Featured Application: The results of this study provide practical guidelines for the design and deployment of artificial intelligence systems on ultra-low-power edge devices (TinyML). By identifying Pareto-efficient configurations across multiple platforms, the proposed framework enables practitioners to select model–platform combinations that best satisfy specific constraints on accuracy, latency, and memory. From a design perspective, the results indicate that lightweight models such as multinomial logistic regression are well-suited for latency-critical and memory-constrained applications, while convolutional neural networks become preferable when higher classification accuracy is required, and additional resources are available. This facilitates informed decision-making in early-stage system design, reducing the need for extensive trial-and-error experimentation. The observed coupling between latency and memory further suggests that optimization strategies should target both resources simultaneously, rather than treating them independently. This insight supports the adoption of joint optimization techniques such as quantization, pruning, and hardware-aware model selection. These guidelines are directly applicable to real-world embedded AI scenarios, including environmental monitoring, precision agriculture, industrial predictive maintenance, and wearable health systems, where strict constraints on computational resources, energy consumption, and cost must be balanced against predictive performance. Deploying artificial intelligence models on low-cost edge devices requires balancing predictive accuracy with strict constraints on computational resources, such as inference latency and memory footprint. Despite growing interest in TinyML systems, limited empirical evidence exists on how these factors interact across different embedded hardware platforms. This study presents a systematic multi-objective evaluation of three lightweight AI architectures—multinomial logistic regression (MLR), multilayer perceptron (MLP), and a reduced convolutional neural network (CNN)—implemented natively on three representative platforms: ESP32-S3, Raspberry Pi Pico, and Raspberry Pi Zero W. The models were evaluated on three image classification datasets of increasing complexity (Synthetic Geometric Figures, MNIST, and Fashion-MNIST), measuring classification accuracy, inference latency, and peak memory footprint under real execution conditions. Pareto-front analysis was used to identify efficient model–platform configurations and characterize the trade-offs between predictive performance and computational resources. The results provide quantitative insight into accuracy–resource trade-offs and establish a reproducible framework for evaluating lightweight AI models on resource-constrained edge devices, supporting informed design decisions in TinyML applications. © 2026 by the authors.
