AB  - Accurate indoor air quality (IAQ) forecasting is essential for smart building management. Conventional forecasting models often suffer from physical inconsistency, phase lag, and excessive computational overhead when deployed on resourceconstrained IoT devices. To address these challenges, we propose PhyRes-IAQ, a physics-informed gated residual network for efficient edge-side IAQ forecasting. The proposed model adopts a state-dependent gray-box architecture that integrates a PhysicsConsistent Evolution (PCE) branch, derived from first-order mass-balance equations, with a Gated Neural Residual Compensation(NRC) branch. In addition, we derive an analytical stepwise solution for the physical evolution process, eliminating the need for numerical ODE solvers and thereby reducing deployment overhead. Experiments on three real-world datasets show that PhyRes-IAQ achieves the best overall RMSE performance. The embedded physical constraints further improve robustness under sparse-data cold-start settings. When deployed on an ESP32S3 microcontroller, the model requires only 42.0 K parameters, with a model size of 164.06 KB, runtime memory of 169.9 KB, and a throughput of 1.76 FPS. These results demonstrate that PhyRes-IAQ provides a lightweight and practical solution for decentralized environmental intelligence. © 2026 IEEE.
