AD  - Jerash University, Department of Cyber Security, Jerash, Jordan
AB  - Fine-tuning large language models (LLMs) remains computationally expensive, limiting access and creating a barrier to broader participation in natural language processing both in research and industry. In this study, we present a sustainable evaluation framework that systematically compares seven fine-tuning configurations representing five distinct adaptation paradigms across three benchmark tasks A total of 63 experimental runs, spanning seven configurations, three tasks, and three random seeds, were conducted on a single NVIDIA T4 GPU with 16 GB of memory under fully controlled and documented hardware and software conditions. Our results reveal an interaction between adaptation capacity, supervision strategy, and dataset size. In particular, a hybrid Knowledge Distillation and Low-Rank Adaptation (KD+LoRA) approach achieves performance comparable to full fine-tuning on the data-rich task (93.35% vs 92.78%), while requiring only 1.39% of the trainable parameters and approximately 45% of the peak memory. However, this advantage decreases on smaller datasets, where the limited capacity of adapter-based methods may constrain effective adaptation. Additionally, learned rescaling through (IA) $ 3$ demonstrates strong performance in low-data settings, outperforming LoRA on two of the three evaluated tasks while training approximately $187 ×$ fewer parameters. Additionally, resource analysis further indicates that reductions in memory usage do not scale proportionally to wall-clock gains. However, parameter-efficient methods can still significantly reduce estimated $CO2$ emissions compared with full fine-tuning. Overall, these findings provide actionable guidance for selecting adaptation strategies under consumer constrained resources and establish a sustainable benchmark for efficient and accessible NLP experimentation.  © 2026 IEEE.
