AD  - Kft Bilişim Sistemleri A.Ş., R&d and Innovation Department, Istanbul, Turkey
AB  - Rapid advancements in the field of artificial intelligence (AI), particularly the dramatic increase in the scalability of Large Language Models (LLMs), have led to significant demands on computational and memory resources during model training and fine-tuning processes. This situation has challenged the sustainability of traditional fine-tuning approaches and highlighted the need for new adaptation strategies. In this context, Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) and its derivatives, which fall under Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) methods, offer innovative solutions that optimize the balance between resource efficiency and model performance. While LoRA enables modular and fast adaptability by using low-rank adapter matrices, QLoRA ensures accessibility on resource-constrained systems by reducing memory consumption through quantization techniques. In contrast, Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation (DoRA) and QDoRA stand out with architectural and mathematical improvements designed to meet accuracy and performance requirements. This study systematically examines the theoretical foundations, architectural mechanisms, and performance metrics of LoRA and its variants, highlighting the critical role of infrastructure constraints, task requirements, and operational priorities in determining successful adaptation strategies for natural language processing models.  © 2026 IEEE.
