AD  - School of Instrumentation and Optoelectronic Engineering, Beihang University, Beijing, 100191, China
AD  - China Federation of Internet Societies, Beijing, 100048, China
AB  - Pre-impact fall detection aims to recognize fall-related motions within the short interval before body impact, requiring both reliable early recognition and efficient on-device inference. However, existing deep learning methods often incur high latency due to complex temporal feature extraction. This letter presents STIR, a bio-inspired stimulus-triggered internal recovery filter for lightweight IMU-based pre-impact fall detection. STIR generates temporal response features through acceleration stimuli and internal state recovery, which are then fed into a lightweight MLP classifier for recognition. On the SisFall dataset, the proposed STIR-based model achieves 97.72% test accuracy with only 26.3k FLOPs. Compared with the lightweight 1DCNN baseline, STIR reduces FLOPs by 97.0% and inference latency by 91.3%. On the STM32U575RIT6 MCU, it achieves an average inference time of 2.6 ms with an estimated energy consumption of 26.32 μJ, while requiring only 2.56 KB of peak RAM and 49.29 KB of Flash storage. These results suggest that STIR has potential for real-time IMU-based pre-impact fall detection on resource-constrained wearable devices. © 2026 IEEE. All rights reserved.
