TI  - A holistic framework for privacy-preserving and trusted cyber threat intelligence in industry 5.0: A fusion of blockchain and tiny semi-supervised class-incremental learning with catastrophic forgetting mitigation
PY  - 2026
T2  - Internet of Things (The Netherlands)
VL  - 37
C7  - 101899
DO  - 10.1016/j.iot.2026.101899
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034528761?origin=resultslist
AD  - Computer Science Department, Faculty of Sciences, Mohammed V University in Rabat, Rabat, 10100, Morocco
AD  - National School of Applied Sciences, Ibn Tofail University, Kenitra, 65800, Morocco
AD  - Center for Ubiquitous Computing, University of Oulu, Oulu, 90570, Finland
AD  - National Higher School for Computer Science and Systems Analysis, Mohammed V University in Rabat, Rabat, 10100, Morocco
AB  - Industry 5.0 relies on intelligent Industrial IoT (IIoT) to promote human-machine collaboration and enhance agility and innovation in manufacturing. Yet, the massive volume of sensitive operational data greatly expands the attack surface to advanced cyber threats. This highlights the need for Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI) that is both privacy-preserving and trustworthy, while operating directly on resource-constrained IIoT devices. Existing TinyML-based CTI approaches remain limited by scarce labeled data and catastrophic forgetting during continual updates. Ensuring the quality and trustworthiness of shared intelligence also remains critical for decision making in collaborative cybersecurity efforts.To address these limitations, we propose a novel holistic CTI framework for Industry 5.0 that fuses Blockchain with a scalable and efficient Tiny Semi-Supervised Class-Incremental Learning model. On the learning side, a confidence-based pseudo-labeling mechanism combined with lightweight generative feature replay enables IIoT devices to support incremental threat detection while preserving past knowledge under strict memory and energy constraints. On the trust and governance side, three Smart Contracts enforce device and collaborator authentication using lightweight cryptography and zk-SNARK proofs, validate CTI quality against predefined criteria, and provide detailed and auditable access control, while offloading encrypted CTI records to IPFS.Extensive experiments on realistic IIoT threat datasets achieve detection accuracies above 98% and memory retention around 96%, with low resource footprint and rapid inference on microcontroller hardware. The Blockchain layer shows cost-efficient operations compared to state-of-the-art designs, demonstrating that the proposed framework offers a practical path toward privacy-preserving and trusted CTI for Industry 5.0 ecosystems. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
