AB  - This paper presents a security architecture motivated by established cryptographic principles and experimentally validated on a resource-constrained Internet of Things platform while preserving cryptographic robustness on embedded nodes. Unlike prior lightweight IoT authentication schemes relying on confidentiality-only encryption, the proposed framework employs AES-GCM as an AEAD primitive, provides replay protection, and defines key derivation using hardware-backed keystores. Experimental evaluation on an Arduino-class microcontroller demonstrates that authenticated encryption and session validation can be achieved within strict RAM and CPU constraints, with negligible latency overhead. The results indicate that secure offloading of identity verification to a trusted mobile device provides a scalable and deployable security model for low-cost IoT environments.  © 2026 IEEE.
