AD  - Department of Computer Science, Mustansiriyah University, Baghdad, Iraq
AB  - Wireless Body Sensor Networks (WBSNs) form the sensing layer of Internet-connected healthcare monitoring services. However, standardized cryptographic algorithms (AES-128-CTR, AES-GCM) impose energy overheads incompatible with the micro-watt power budgets of medical sensor nodes. To address this, this paper presents GreenSec-WBSN, implemented as a novel Embedded WBAN Context Middleware optimized for 32-bit microcontrollers. The proposed architecture fundamentally decouples key derivation from authenticated data encryption through a pipelined execution model across three distinct phases: context initialization utilizing hardware-optimized reduced-round ASCON-8 (NIST SP 800–232, 8 permutation rounds) for session key generation, followed by pipelined payload encryption using LEA-5 (ISO/IEC 29192–2, 5 rounds) in counter mode, and concluding with an ASCON-based MAC generation. Round reductions are justified under a formally defined bounded threat model where WBSN data is short-lived, per-key volumes remain below 218 blocks per epoch, and keys rotate every 10 min. The state-driven design securely manages the cryptographic Context State (S) within a strict 112-byte RAM boundary, eliminating dynamic memory allocation overheads. An experimental evaluation on an ARM Cortex-M4 (80 MHz) with 1000 trials per measurement demonstrates: authenticated encryption at 12.8 µJ per block (82.4% reduction vs. AES-GCM), a throughput of 1070 cycles/block, and a battery lifetime extension to 28.1 h for continuous ECG monitoring. All baselines are benchmarked under identical conditions with cross-layer hardware mapping constraints. © 2026 The Author(s)
