TI  - Adaptive Code Relocation: Mitigating Remote Brute-Force Code Gadgets in Small IoT Devices
PY  - 2025
T2  - ACM CPSS 2025 - Proceedings of the 11th ACM Cyber-Physical System Security Workshop
SP  - 31
EP  - 42
DO  - 10.1145/3709017.3737707
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105020388110?origin=resultslist
AD  - Viet Nam
AD  - Faculty of Computer Science and Mathematics, Germany
AB  - IoT devices are typically built on microcontrollers with constrained computational and memory resources. To minimize development complexity and cost, firmware on these devices often relies on static linker scripts that define fixed memory layouts and offer minimal access control across memory regions. This results in predictable memory organization and weak memory isolation, making the system vulnerable to code reuse attacks. Adversaries can exploit these weaknesses by manipulating network packets to remotely brute-force and trigger the execution of reusable code gadgets. In this paper, we analyze security in linker scripts available in IoT platforms and demonstrate how they can be exploited for remote code execution. To mitigate these risks, we propose an adaptive code relocation mechanism that dynamically adjusts function placement based on network traffic. Our approach is implemented in Contiki-NG to evaluate across various function extraction scenarios and unload/reload strategies. Results show that while it incurs high overhead in some cases, dynamic threshold adjustments effectively balance security and performance, making it a viable solution for securing small IoT devices, typically, running network applications.  © 2025 ACM.
