AB  - The paper introduces DPNet, a lightweight and privacy-preserving acoustic sensing framework designed for real-time bathroom sound classification on resource-constrained microcontrollers. Traditional camera-based or wearable systems compromise user privacy and consume high power, while many existing audio classification methods struggle with noise robustness and limited on-device inference capabilities. DPNet addresses these challenges through a microcontroller-optimized deep learning architecture deployed on the ESP32-S3 Nano, equipped with 512 KB SRAM and 8 MB PSRAM. The system leverages a compact convolutional network and minimal preprocessing to classify seven bathroom activities, including toilet flushing, showering, and tap running, without external or cloud dependency. The proposed framework introduces three core innovations: (i) a Temporal Feature Extraction Block (TFEB) that reduces parameters while maintaining accuracy; (ii) mixed-precision quantization (int8-float32) for memory-efficient inference on the device; and (iii) real-time feedback integration for verifications. Experimental evaluations on an in-house dataset demonstrate that DPNet achieves 99.21% accuracy on high-performance processors and 73.44% real-time accuracy on the ESP32-S3 Nano, outperforming existing models such as ACDNet in both compactness and deployability. © 2026 IEEE.
