AB  - The advent of quantum computing necessitates a paradigm shift in securing Firmware-Over-The-Air (FOTA) updates for Internet of Things (IoT) devices. To address this issue, we propose and evaluate a novel quantum-resistant FOTA architecture that integrates three key technologies: The CRYSTALS-Dilithium-5 signature scheme for post-quantum authentication, leveraging an Ethereum smart contract for immutable firmware registration, and the InterPlanetary File System (IPFS) for resilient distribution. Our prototype, benchmarked over 1,000 iterations in a browser environment, validates the practicality of this approach. We measured an average signature verification time of 0.1214 ms for Dilithium, providing 192-bit post-quantum security while incurring a manageable 1.5 × performance overhead compared to the quantum-vulnerable ECDSA P-256 standard. While publisher-side signing incurs a 7.7 × overhead, yet the sub-millisecond verification confirms client-side feasibility. This paper presents the full system design, a comprehensive performance analysis, and discusses adapting this model to embedded Linux platforms (e.g., Raspberry Pi) and the challenges for resource-constrained microcontrollers.  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
