AB  - The rapid expansion of the Internet of Things (IoT) and digital logistics has created a critical demand for machine-readable data carriers that are both high density and resource-efficient. While standard 2-D quick response (QR) codes are ubiquitous, they suffer from inherent capacity limitations. The four-state QR code (4SQR code) addresses this by utilizing four distinct geometric shapes to increase data density significantly. However, decoding such complex patterns introduces computational overhead that challenges existing mobile and edge hardware. To bridge this gap, this article introduces 4SQR-CodeScanner, a lightweight decoding framework designed for resource-constrained environments. Unlike traditional deep learning approaches that rely on heavy runtime libraries, the proposed system utilizes a new zero-overhead inference (ZOI) mechanism. By directly loading the weights and biases of a trained artificial neural network (ANN) and performing inference via optimized matrix operations, the decoder eliminates the dependency on deep learning frameworks during inference. The architecture is designed to be compatible with transpilation for bare-metal microcontrollers, making it a viable candidate for extending the 4SQR ecosystem to low-power industrial IoT nodes. To validate the proposed approach, the complete decoding pipeline was deployed on a Raspberry Pi 3 Model B, achieving 100% decoding accuracy on both synthetic and real-world captured datasets with a memory footprint of less than 10 MB. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed system achieves high decoding capability while reducing peak memory consumption to approximately 6 MB and decoding time to approximately 65 ms on a desktop workstation. This represents a significant efficiency gain over the baseline method and convolutional neural network (CNN) models like MobileNetV2, validating 4SQR-CodeScanner as a viable decoder for high-capacity 4SQR codes.  © 2014 IEEE.
