AB  - The rapid expansion of e-Health demands robust security measures for the transmission of sensitive biomedical data over public networks. In this work, we present a real-time chaos-based encryption system for electrocardiogram signals, specifically optimized for resource-constrained embedded architectures. An additive feedback encryption algorithm is proposed, which uses a coupled system of three Hénon maps to generate a permutation vector and a diffusion mask, implemented on a low-cost 32-bit microcontroller to protect electrocardiogram signals for e-Health applications. Experimental results are presented that validate the system’s efficiency, achieving a key space robust against brute-force attacks, information entropy close to ideal noise, and zero correlation between plaintext and ciphertext, as well as high sensitivity to plaintext, making it robust against differential attacks. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier B.V.
