UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048233791?origin=resultslist
AD  - Indira Gandhi Delhi Technical University for Women, Kashmere Gate, Delhi, 110006, India
AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong performance across a wide range of natural language understanding and generation tasks; however, fully fine-tuning such models is computationally expensive and memory-intensive. Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) methods offer a practical alternative by updating only a small subset of model parameters while retaining most of the capabilities of the base model. In this work, we present a comprehensive empirical comparison of five popular PEFT techniques; LoRA, QLoRA, BitFit, Prefix Tuning, and Prompt Tuning, applied to multiple education-oriented NLP benchmarks, including SciQ, RACE, and SQuAD. Using a 3B-parameter instruction-tuned LLM as the base model, we systematically evaluate each method under identical training conditions and compute constraints. Our experiments measure accuracy, BERTScore, ROUGE-L, F1 Score, training time, GPU memory usage, number of trainable parameters etc. for evaluating tasks and training efficiency. With the objective to provide fair and controlled comparisons under limited resource constraints, the pipeline integrates quantized model loading, adapter-based fine-tuning, automatic metric evaluation, dataset pre-processing, and comprehensive GPU memory monitoring. Besides providing a reproducible and expandable framework for future research on lightweight LLM adaptation, the study provides practical insights into choosing the best PEFT technique for instructional NLP tasks and low-resource environments. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
