AB  - Outdoor air monitoring for possible air pollution is always one of the important tasks in managing our living environment. A module of five gas sensors and a new learning model is developed by this study to provide precision, real-time multiple gas concentrations in public spaces. The module suits well an Internet of Things (IoT) node of smart gas sensing that integrates a custom-designed sensor array, driving circuits, and wireless transmission via Bluetooth/Wi-Fi. The sensed gas data are uploaded to a cloud platform for remote storage and then retrieved by an edge device for on-site processing. A cascaded multilayer perceptron (MLP) and long short-term memory (LSTM) model, deployed at the edge using TensorFlow Lite for Microcontrollers (TFLM), compensates successfully for sensor cross-sensitivity, temperature and humidity variations, and other environmental interferences, leading to precise multiple-to-multiple gas concentration estimation. This edge AI deployment reduces cloud computation load and makes it possible to an inference in resource-constrained environments. Experimental validation demonstrates that the proposed MLP–LSTM approach achieves accuracies with mean absolute errors (MAEs) within 113 ppb, 0.108 ppm, 0.110 ppm, 0.124 ppm, and 0.13 ppb for NOx, H2S, TVOC, CO, and SO2, respectively, superior to those by traditional analysis methods. The system architecture also shows great capability for future integration into IoT applications and an alert system for environmental air monitoring. © 2014 IEEE.
