AB  - Currently, the increasing number of Edge computing devices generates massive volumes of data, demanding highly efficient local storage. Traditional databases cause excessive wear on the non-volatile memory (Flash), while consuming unpredictable amounts of volatile memory (SRAM). Furthermore, existing mathematical indices are highly vulnerable to hardware anomalies and noise. The proposal in this paper aims to overcome these problems by implementing an architecture combining a learned index with a real-time algorithmic filter for data anomalies. An experiment was conducted on a resource-constrained microcontroller with continuous exposure to intense sensor data. The proposed architecture demonstrates significantly reduced write latency, resulting in minimal RAM resource consumption and drastically reduced wear on the Flash memory.  © 2026 IEEE.
