AD  - National School of Electronics and Telecommunications, CES Laboratory, Sfax, Tunisia
AD  - Enet'Com, CES Laboratory, Sfax, Tunisia
AB  - The rapid growth of Internet of Things (IoT) healthcare systems enables continuous monitoring of chronic diseases such as diabetes, but also raises challenges related to limited computational resources, energy efficiency, and cybersecurity. This paper presents a secure embedded artificial intelligence (AI)-based diabetes monitoring system implemented on the STM32F407-DISCO microcontroller with an integrated machine learning-based Intrusion Detection System (IDS). A lightweight deep learning model is developed using a public diabetes dataset comprising eight physiological features and optimized for deployment on resource-constrained embedded hardware. To enhance system security, an IDS is incorporated to detect abnormal or malicious network traffic that could compromise medical data and clinical decisions. The proposed architecture combines on-device health monitoring, real-Time AI inference, and cybersecurity protection within a single embedded platform. Experimental results demonstrate that the system achieves reliable diabetes classification accuracy with low computational and memory overhead. Furthermore, the IDS significantly improves system resilience against cyber threats, ensuring data integrity, patient privacy, and operational reliability. These results validate the feasibility of secure, real-Time IoT healthcare monitoring on low-power embedded devices.  © 2026 IEEE.
