AB  - Automatic text summarization is essential for managing the rapid growth of textual data in specialized domains like law and finance. Recent transformer architectures have significantly improved the performance of abstractive text summarization systems. Conventional full fine-tuning (FFT) requires significant computational resources and memory, limiting practical deployment. This study investigates and compares full fine-tuning with parameter-efficient approaches, specifically Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) and Prefix Tuning, for the summarization of long legal documents. Using the BART-base architecture on the BillSum dataset, we evaluate these methods based on summarization quality (ROUGE and BERTScore) and computational efficiency. Our results demonstrate that while FFT achieves the highest performance (ROUGE-L of 0.3151), LoRA achieves highly competitive results (ROUGE-L of 0.3055) while updating only 0.32% of the parameters and reducing peak GPU memory usage by over 60%. Prefix Tuning offers the highest efficiency with the lowest memory footprint but at a higher cost to performance. These findings establish PEFT as a efficient, scalable alternative to full fine-tuning for domain-specific summarization in resource-constrained environments. © 2026 IEEE.
