AB  - This systematic review analyzes the strategic convergence of Artificial Intelligence (AI), advanced hardware architecture, and the Controller Area Network (CAN) communication protocol to optimize the performance and sustainability of lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) in electric vehicles (EVs). Unlike previous literature reviews that typically isolate software algorithms from hardware constraints, the novel contribution of this paper is the holistic evaluation of this hardware-software-communication triad and its direct alignment with global sustainability targets. Guided by a methodology aligned with the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), a comprehensive analysis of recent literature published between 2019 and 2026 was conducted. The study evaluates how the implementation of deep learning algorithms (DL), such as Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) and Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN), enables precise estimation of the State of Charge (SOC) and State of Health (SOH), surmounting the limitations of traditional methods in dynamic operational environments. The findings demonstrate that hardware optimization through quantization techniques drastically reduces resource consumption and minimizes inference time. This efficiency facilitates the execution of complex models on resource-constrained Microcontroller Units (MCUs) within the Battery Management System (BMS). The study concludes that this technological synergy is instrumental in achieving targets for affordable and clean energy (SDG 7) and industrial innovation (SDG 9).  © 2013 IEEE.
