UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041638955?origin=resultslist
AD  - Nit Agartala, Tripura, India
AD  - Nit Agartala, Dept. of Cse, Tripura, India
AB  - Lightweight cryptography has become essential for securing data in resource constrained platforms such as IoT sensor nodes, RFID tags, and embedded microcontrollers. Classical ciphers like Caesar and Vigenere are computationally efficient but exhibit weak confusion, low entropy, and almost zero avalanche, making them unsuitable for modern security requirements. This paper proposes a hybrid lightweight stream cipher that combines a Caesar-based modular addition with a key-dependent AES S-Box substitution and an ARX-driven keystream generator. The design introduces nonlinear confusion and per-byte diffusion while maintaining a low computational footprint suitable for 8-bit and 16-bit microcontrollers. This work shows that the proposed cipher significantly improves ciphertext randomness, achieving 7.21 bits/byte entropy and improved avalanche effect compared to classical ciphers. Hardware estimations show that the cipher requires approximately 450-550 Gate Equivalents (GEs), which is much lower than AES (3400-5000 GEs) and PRESENT (1570 GEs). These results indicate that the hybrid cipher provides a practical balance between security and computational efficiency, making it suitable for lightweight IoT environments. © 2026 IEEE.
