TI  - A Systematic Review of TinyML Techniques for Efficient Inference and On-Device Learning
PY  - 2026
T2  - IEEE Access
DO  - 10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3736856
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105051469264?origin=resultslist
AD  - Georgia Institute of Technology, Atlanta, 30332, GA, United States
AB  - Tiny Machine Learning enables inference and, increasingly, learning directly on microcontrollers and other resource-constrained devices. Its relevance has grown with the expansion of physical artificial intelligence systems, where on-device execution removes the dependence on continuous network connectivity and reduces latency. TinyML systems operate under strict memory, computing, and energy constraints, making efficient model design a primary requirement. As a result, research on optimization techniques that maintain accuracy under these constraints has expanded rapidly in recent years. This paper presents a systematic review of 156 TinyML studies published between 2023 and 2025, organized across model compression, execution-level optimization, hardware-software co-design, system-level methods, and on-device adaptation. On-device adaptation, which accounts for 40 of the 156 reviewed papers, is treated as a primary category, spanning backpropagation-free learning, unsupervised and self-supervised adaptation, continual and incremental learning, transfer and few-shot adaptation, and federated learning. The methods are analyzed with respect to hardware constraints and deployment tradeoffs. This review identifies recurring design patterns, highlights underexplored assumptions, and outlines open challenges for edge deployment. © 2013 IEEE.
