TI  - TinyML-based Edge Intelligence With Real-Time Earth Tremor Detection In Low-Power IoT Devices
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Advanced Computing and Knowledge Engineering, ICACKE 2026
DO  - 10.1109/ICACKE65470.2026.11651100
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048478900?origin=resultslist
AD  - GITAM Deemed To Be University, Dept. of Computer Science and Engineering, Bangalore, India
AB  - Traditional earthquake monitoring systems are based on centralized seismic monitoring sites and highperformance computing facilities, which can add response lags and scale limitation in resource-constrained areas. To overcome these shortcomings, the paper examines the possibility of implementing deep learning to detect earthquakes on lowpower embedded hardware in Tiny Machine Learning (TinyML). The ESP32 microcontroller together with a sensor to measure acceleration, MPU6050, were planned as the underlying components of a real-time earthquake detection system. Data of seismic waveforms of the Mendeley miniSEED dataset were initially processed with signal conditioning methods such as down-sampling, normalization and sliding window segmentation to produce fixed length time-series inputs. The model that was trained is 1D Convolutional Neural Network (1D-CNN) to classify earthquakes and then transformed it into TensorFlow Lite Micro to be inferred on-device. The optimized code was converted into C-compatible code to be deployed on the embedded memory of the microcontroller allowing standalone processing in realtime, without depending on cloud infrastructure. Experimental evaluation demonstrated that the proposed system achieved an accuracy of approximately 83%, with performance assessed using precision, recall, F1-score, and confusion matrix metrics. The implemented system was able to effectively detect earthquakes continuously and with a low latency and consuming minimal power as well as be able to offer trustworthy confidence-based alerts through an LCD interface. The outcomes confirm that it is possible to deploy deep learning models on microcontroller-class machines to perform distributed seismic surveillance and smart cities.  © 2026 IEEE.
