AD  - SCYTALE Group, Computer Engineering and Informatics Department, University of Patras, Patras, Greece
AB  - Tiny machine learning (TinyML) models are increasingly deployed in resource-limited applications like healthcare, industrial monitoring, and smart cities, making security and privacy essential. This work addresses the unique challenges of securing TinyML models on microcontrollers and low-power devices vulnerable to cyber- and physical threats. A layered security approach combines hardware and software solutions to protect model integrity and data privacy. Privacy-preserving model updates are enabled through federated learning frameworks, and adversarial robustness is enhanced with adversarial training and defensive distillation. Additionally, hardware protections, including physically unclonable functions, anti-tampering mechanisms, lightweight encryption, and trusted execution environments, safeguard data from unauthorized access. Real-time threat detection supports secure, continuous monitoring, enabling TinyML models to operate reliably within privacy-sensitive Internet of Things deployments. © 2026 by The Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers, Inc. All rights reserved.
