AB  - Internet of Things (IoT) applications like smart cities, environmental monitoring, and precision agriculture, LoRaWAN networks are a crucial low-power, long-range communication technology. They can establish longdistance connections between a large number of battery-operated sensory devices. Energy efficiency is crucial for network continuity because these devices have limited power and processing capabilities. By forecasting signal quality, controlling power, planning transmissions, and identifying interference, deep learning has improved the performance of LoRaWAN networks. However, conventional deep learning models may not be suitable for resource-constrained environments due to their high power and computational requirements. Due to their low power consumption, rapid execution, and ability to run locally, lightweight Deep Learning (DL) models can be utilized to function effectively in LPWAN networks. In this paper, the TinyCNN, GRU, and the hybrid TinyCNN-GRU are proposed and evaluated to forecast the value of the SNR. In LoRaWAN, predicting SNR allows for dynamic transmission power and data rate adjustment, which leads to reduced energy consumption while preserving dependable communication. To balance the complexity and the model accuracy, the Gray Wolf Optimization (GWO) algorithm is used to determine the best values for model parameters, such as learning rate, number of layers, and filter size. The obtained results using time-based leakage-free evaluation, show that the proposed optimized hybrid TinyCNN-GRU achieved a balance between the accuracy in terms of R2 (0.980) and RMSE (0.242), while the model complexity is minimized in terms of number of FLOPs, inference time, model size, and memory usage. The simulation results of LoRaWAN networks demonstrate that the proposed method outperformed the traditional approaches including, Adaptive Data Rate (ADR) and the heuristic in terms of airtime and energy minimization. The optimized TinyCNN-GRU model is more suitable for LoRaWAN applications by extending the lifespan of sensor nodes and enhancing system dependability. © This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License. License details: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
