AD  - National Polytechnic University of Armenia, Yerevan, Armenia
AB  - The proliferation of Internet of Things (IoT) devices has introduced significant security and resiliency conformance challenges, particularly when working towards implementing continuous Software Bill of Material (SBOM) and Supply Chain Risk Management measures, as well as Zero Trust Network Access management systems and Zero Trust Architecture (ZTA) principles. RFC 9783, published in June 2025, specifies the Platform Security Architecture (PSA) Attestation Token as a standardized framework for hardware- rooted device attestation. This paper presents a comprehensive performance analysis and security evaluation of RFC 9783 PSA attestation tokens implemented on resource-constrained IoT devices. We evaluate the computational overhead, memory footprint, and network transmission costs of PSA token generation and verification across multiple hardware platforms including ARM CortexM series microcontrollers. Our experimental results demonstrate that PSA attestation introduces an average overhead of 23.4 ms for token generation and 18.7 ms for verification on ARM Cortex-M33 devices, with a memory footprint of 4.2 KB for token processing. Security analysis reveals that PSA tokens provide robust protection against replay attacks, man-in-the-middle attacks, and device cloning while maintaining acceptable performance for real- time IoT applications. We identify optimization strategies that reduce attestation overhead by 31% through caching mechanisms and propose enhancements to the privacy- preserving capabilities of the PSA framework. © 2026 IEEE.
