AD  - International Ph.D. Program in Innovative Technology of Biomedical Engineering and Medical Devices, Ming Chi University of Technology, New Taipei City, Taiwan
AB  - This paper presents a TinyML-enabled intelligent aeration control framework for aquaculture environments based on a quantized Deep Q-Network (DQN) policy designed for resource-constrained embedded platforms. Unlike existing deep reinforcement learning–based aquaculture control systems that rely on cloud computation or high-power edge devices, the proposed approach enables fully autonomous, low-latency on-device inference on a microcontroller-class platform. A physics-constrained simulation environment, AquaSim–Taiwan, incorporating diurnal cycles, seasonal variations, and stochastic disturbances is developed to train and evaluate the control policy offline. The trained DQN model is quantized using INT8 post-training quantization and deployed on a Raspberry Pi Pico (RP2040) microcontroller, achieving an inference latency of approximately 3.2 ms per forward pass with a memory footprint of 20.4 KB Flash and 6.1 KB SRAM. Simulation-based evaluation demonstrates that the proposed controller maintains dissolved oxygen within biologically safe thresholds of 4–5 mg/L while achieving an estimated mean 65% reduction in relative aeration energy usage (0.35 ± 0.33 across five evaluation episodes) compared to continuous full-power operation computed from controller actuation patterns, not direct electrical power measurements. Simulation fidelity is further validated through distributional comparison against real-world pond measurements of dissolved oxygen, pH, and oxidation–reduction potential collected from a commercial aquaculture site in Tainan, Taiwan, confirming realistic multi-parameter physicochemical dynamics. These findings collectively demonstrate the feasibility of deploying a quantized DRL policy on TinyML-class microcontroller hardware; full closed-loop validation under real pond conditions remains the subject of future work. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
