AB  - The increasing size of large language models (LLMs) has significantly improved natural language understanding and generation; however, it has also exacerbated the computational complexity of fine-tuning. This paper proposes PrunedLoRA, a lightweight and parameter-efficient fine-tuning methodology that combines structured pruning with low-rank adaptation (LoRA) to enhance training efficiency and reduce resource consumption. The proposed method employs a gradient-based pruning procedure to identify and remove low-impact parameters during the adaptation phase, thereby reducing both memory usage and computational cost while preserving model accuracy. Experimental evaluations across various NLP tasks, including machine translation, sentiment analysis, text classification, and named-entity recognition, demonstrate that PrunedLoRA achieves over 60% faster training and reduces memory usage by nearly half compared to traditional fine-tuning and baseline LoRA approaches. The results confirm that the proposed framework maintains comparable accuracy while delivering significant improvements in efficiency and scalability. Owing to its compact and robust design, PrunedLoRA provides a practical solution for deploying LLMs in resource-constrained or edge-AI environments. Overall, the findings highlight the potential of PrunedLoRA to advance efficient adaptation of LLMs for real-time applications in both industrial and academic settings.  © 2026 IEEE.
