AB  - This chapter explores privacy-preserving techniques for tiny machine learning (TinyML), addressing the unique challenges posed by resource-constrained Internet of Things (IoT) devices. We examine state-of-the-art approaches in secure multi-party computation (MPC), homomorphic encryption (HE), and differential privacy (DP) tailored for TinyML environments. The chapter provides a comprehensive analysis of frameworks such as SecureML, SecureNN, and Falcon, highlighting their contributions to efficient and secure computation in limited-resource settings. We discuss the trade-offs between security guarantees, computational efficiency, and communication overhead, which are crucial for TinyML applications. The evaluation of these frameworks on various neural network architectures and datasets demonstrates significant performance improvements, making privacy-preserving TinyML more practical for real-world deployment. Key considerations, including performance-security trade-offs, communication efficiency, energy consumption, model accuracy, and scalability, are addressed. The chapter concludes by identifying promising future research directions, including hardware-software co-design for privacy-preserving operations, secure federated learning for TinyML, and privacy-preserving transfer learning. This work provides a foundation for researchers and practitioners to understand and advance privacy-preserving techniques in the rapidly evolving field of TinyML. © 2026 by The Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers, Inc. All rights reserved.
