AD  - Astana It University, School of Cybersecurity, Astana, 010000, Kazakhstan
AB  - Wireless IoT-based LED display systems are widely deployed in public and commercial environments due to their low cost and operational flexibility. This paper presents the design, implementation, and security assessment of a programmable LED display system comprising an Arduino Nano microcontroller, an HC-05 Bluetooth module, four MAX7219-controlled 8×8 LED matrices forming an 8×32 display, and a companion Android application built with MIT App Inventor. Security analysis reveals critical vulnerabilities in the Bluetooth communication layer: the HC-05 operates with factory-default credentials, transmits data in plaintext over an unauthenticated SPP channel, and remains discoverable to any proximate device. A simulated penetration test confirmed that an attacker within 10 meters can pair via the default PIN '1234' and inject arbitrary messages in under 5 seconds without specialized tooling. Applying the STRIDE threat model and OWASP IoT Top 10 framework, we identify spoofing, tampering, and information disclosure as the principal threat categories. We propose three lightweight countermeasures - PIN hardening, HMAC-SHA256 message authentication with replay protection, and firmware input validation - validated as feasible on the ATmega328P: the HMAC implementation incurs 12.4 ms overhead per 64-byte message, 2.4 KB Flash (7.5%), and 128 bytes SRAM (6.25%). Our findings argue about security-by-design at every price point in the IoT ecosystem. © 2026 IEEE.
