AD  - Ningbo University, School of Integrated Circuits, Ningbo, China
AB  - Existing pressure-based sitting posture recognition methods often rely on high-performance computing platforms, limiting deployment on resource-constrained microcontrollers (MCUs). This paper presents an embedded posture recognition system integrating a 32 × 32 flexible pressure sensor array, a lightweight convolutional neural network (LW-CNN) with a Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) attention module, and a hierarchical differential channel pruning strategy. The proposed LW-CNN achieves 96.41% baseline accuracy on a PC platform. To enable deployment on the STM32H743 MCU, different pruning ratios are assigned to shallow and deep convolutional layers, followed by fine-tuning. Experimental results show that the pruned model reduces Flash memory usage by 31.25%, Static Random-Access Memory (SRAM) consumption by 12.50%, and inference latency by 21.40%, while sacrificing only 2.39% accuracy. The deployed model achieves 92.85% accuracy for seven-class sitting posture recognition, demonstrating an effective trade-off between recognition performance and resource efficiency for edge AI applications.  © 2026 IEEE.
