AB  - Web vulnerabilities including Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) and SQL Injection pose persistent security threats. While large language models show promise for vulnerability detection, their computational demands (>7B parameters) hinder practical deployment. This preliminary study investigates parameter-efficient fine-tuning of small models for vulnerability detection in resource-constrained settings. We fine-tune CodeT5-small (220M parameters) with Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) on 800 CVEfixes examples targeting XSS (CWE-79) and SQL injection (CWE-89). The approach achieves F1-score of 0.369 with precision of 0.354, outperforming traditional SAST tools (F1: 0.333) and zero-shot baselines (F1: 0.340). With 880MB model size, <100ms latency, and <2GB GPU memory requirements, the system enables practical CI/CD integration. Results demonstrate proof-of-concept viability for small model-based vulnerability detection, addressing deployment feasibility gaps that limit large model adoption while maintaining competitive detection performance. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
