TI  - A Comparative Benchmark and Decision Guide for Edge AI Deployment: Comparative Study of Rule-Based, Cloud, TinyML, and Federated TinyML on Resource-Constrained IMU Sensor Nodes
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings - 2026 5th ICERA 2026: The International Conference on Electronics Representation and Algorithm
SP  - 904
EP  - 909
DO  - 10.1109/ICERA72709.2026.11666716
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105050628689?origin=resultslist
AD  - FPT University, FPT School of Business and Technology, Hanoi, Viet Nam
AB  - Practitioners deploying intelligence on resource-constrained sensor nodes lack a structured way to choose among rule-based logic, cloud offloading, on-device TinyML, and federated TinyML (FL) under competing constraints (memory, connectivity, privacy, multi-user adaptation). We present a decision guide for edge AI deployment backed by a reproducible four-way benchmark for IMU gesture recognition on Cortex-M4-class hardware. Four paradigms-(A) rule/heuristic, (B) cloud ML, (C) int8 TFLM, and (D) TinyML with PC-coordinated FedAvg-are evaluated under a unified schema: accuracy, macro-F1, latency, RAM peak, flash, per-window bandwidth, and FL communication per round. We use 10 random seeds and 5-fold stratified cross-validation on an 8-class synthetic gesture dataset. Representative test accuracy ranges from 83.6% (A) to 99.6% (B); mean±std over 50 runs per paradigm is 81.1±1.4% (A) through 99.9±0.2% (B). Cloud inference incurs 2,404 B/window uplink and 12.3 ms end-to-end latency (p50); on-device TinyML infers in <0.01 ms (p50) with 6.8 KB flash ([ESTIMATED-MCU]). Federated TinyML reaches 98.4% float test accuracy by round 8 versus 96.1% int8 on the representative split (16.4 KB/round). Contributions: (1) constraint-driven deployment guide, (2) unified evaluation protocol with open artifact, and (3) empirical mean±std evidence-not a new algorithm or on-board measurements. Results reproduce via python scripts/run_benchmark.py - seeds 10. © 2026 IEEE.
