AB  - Effective crop management in modern precision agriculture increasingly depends on the precise, real-time forecasting of multi-dimensional environmental variables. While complex deep learning models often lack deployment feasibility on resource-constrained hardware, there is a critical need for interpretable and computationally efficient systems. This study develops a scalable Multi-Output Linear Regression (MOLR) framework for forecasting key environmental indicators—including air humidity, temperature, atmospheric pressure, soil moisture, light intensity, pH, and water quality. A robust preprocessing pipeline was implemented on the LH_Data dataset, incorporating StandardScaler normalization, median imputation, and interquartile range (IQR) outlier filtering, alongside quantile-based sample weighting to mitigate data imbalance. The MOLR model was evaluated against non-linear benchmarks, including Random Forest and XGBoost. Results demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves high predictive accuracy (R² = 0.979, MAE = 0.486). The interpretability stems from the transparent regression coefficients in the MOLR model, which explicitly reveal the impact of each environmental sensor on the predicted indicators. For practical implementation, the system was validated across two scenarios: a cloud-based API with an end-to-end latency of 0.41 s, and a direct firmware integration on ESP32 microcontrollers. In the edge-only configuration, the model achieved a remarkable inference latency of 28 ms with a negligible memory footprint of 12 KB, confirming its suitability for autonomous, battery-powered Internet of Things (IoT) nodes in remote agricultural environments. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag London Ltd., part of Springer Nature 2026.
