AB  - Cloud-based artificial intelligence (AI) has enabled significant progress in medical image analysis; however, its deployment remains limited by high energy consumption, network latency, privacy concerns, and restricted accessibility in resource-constrained environments. These limitations are particularly critical for medical applications requiring reliable, real-time, and privacy-preserving operation. In this paper, we propose an embedded artificial intelligence approach based on Tiny Machine Learning (TinyML), enabling on-device skin lesion classification without reliance on cloud infrastructure. The proposed system deploys a quantized and structurally pruned MobileNetV2 model on a low-cost microcontroller with severely limited memory resources. Through quantizationaware training and structured pruning, the original 14.1 MB model is reduced to 969 KB and executed entirely on-device. Experimental results on the HAM10000 dataset demonstrate a classification accuracy of 92.7%, a melanoma recall of 93.5%, and an AUC of 0.976. Compared to cloud-based inference, the embedded solution achieves a 12.3 × reduction in energy consumption (0.41 J vs. 5.04 J per inference) while operating fully offline and preserving data privacy. These results demonstrate that clinically relevant AI models can be deployed on ultra-low-power embedded platforms, enabling accessible, energy-efficient, and privacy-preserving intelligent medical systems. © 2026 IEEE.
