AB  - Recent advances in natural language processing have enabled increasingly accurate detection of harmful online communication, including toxic language and online grooming. However, most state-of-the-art approaches rely on cloud-based large language models, raising concerns about latency, computational cost, energy consumption, and especially user privacy. These limitations motivate interest in deploying compact language models directly on edge devices, where inference can be performed locally without transmitting sensitive user data to external servers. This survey reviews small language models for privacy-preserving harmful-content detection on resource-constrained platforms, focusing on toxicity and grooming detection. We examine model families, compression strategies, privacy-preserving deployment, and hardware-aware trade-offs relevant to embedded and microcontroller-class systems, as well as the suitability of existing datasets and evaluation criteria for safety-critical edge NLP. The survey indicates that toxicity detection is approaching practical feasibility on edge hardware, whereas grooming detection remains more challenging due to its stronger dependence on long-context reasoning and conversational dynamics. We finally outline key open challenges and future directions in multilinguality, robustness, explainability, privacy, and standardized benchmarking.  © 2026 IEEE.
