AB  - Intelligent Transportation Systems increasingly rely on data-driven models to support real-time traffic monitoring and control. However, practical deployment remains challenging due to limited computational resources at sensor nodes and the lack of historical data for newly installed sensors. This paper proposes an adaptive traffic prediction framework that integrates Tiny Machine Learning with few-shot adaptation for edge-based deployment. A lightweight one-dimensional convolutional neural network is designed for short-term traffic forecasting and optimized through post-training quantization. The resulting model achieves a memory footprint of only 5.46 KB, enabling execution on low-power microcontroller platforms. To address data scarcity, a two-stage few-shot adaptation strategy is introduced that allows the model to adapt to new traffic environments using a small amount of local data. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed approach achieves an MSE of 564.72 and RMSE of 23.77 on the target domain using only 48 hours of calibration data. The framework significantly reduces communication overhead and energy consumption compared to cloud-based processing. These results confirm the feasibility of accurate and energy-efficient traffic prediction directly on resource-constrained edge devices.  © 2026 IEEE.
