TI  - Resource-Constrained Embedded High-Order Runge-Kutta 7(6) Adaptive Solver for Real-Time Sensor Data Recovery and Reconstruction in Rail Transit IoT Edge Devices
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of 2026 2nd International Conference on IOT, Data Science and Advanced Computing, IDSAC 2026
SP  - 199
EP  - 203
DO  - 10.1109/IDSAC70973.2026.11650625
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049044508?origin=resultslist
AD  - Southwest Jiaotong University Hope College, Sichuan, Chengdu, 610400, China
AD  - School of Education, Fuzhou University of International Studies and Trade, Fuzhou, China
AB  - Rail transit systems rely on continuous sensor data from Internet of Things (IoT) edge devices to ensure safe operations and enable predictive maintenance. However, wireless packet loss frequently compromises data integrity on resource-limited platforms. This paper develops an embedded high-order Runge-Kutta 7(6) adaptive solver for real-Time recovery and reconstruction of missing sensor data directly on the edge device. Existing approaches, including linear interpolation, Kalman filters, and recent lightweight time series reconstruction methods such as edge-deployable convolutional neural networks or recurrent networks, exhibit clear limitations: They either distort high-frequency dynamics or impose computational and memory demands unsuitable for severely constrained IoT hardware with only 64 KB RAM. The proposed solver is based on a Verner-Type 7(6) Runge-Kutta pair with adaptive step-size control using a PI controller. It incorporates Q31 fixed-point arithmetic for deterministic computation on microcontrollers without floating-point units, First-Same-As-Last (FSAL) optimization to reduce function evaluations by 10 \%, in-place memory computation to minimize stack usage to below 1.7 KB, and a rail-specific warm-start strategy that leverages the last valid state and step-size for rapid convergence after burst losses. These techniques enable reliable deployment on ARM (Advanced RISC Machine) Cortex-M and ESP32 platforms using only 64 KB RAM and 256 KB Flash while delivering superior accuracy, spectral fidelity, and efficiency without any model training or cloud dependency. Experiments on real operational high-speed rail and metro datasets demonstrate that the solver achieves a mean relative error (MRE) below 0.65% at 1 kHz sampling rate under realistic packet loss patterns (5-18% random and burst losses up to 600 ms). It outperforms traditional linear interpolation and Kalman filter methods by 3.5-5.5 times in accuracy while keeping the Central Processing Unit (CPU) duty cycle under 23%, with peak RAM usage of only 2.48 KB and Flash footprint of 23.7 KB. This work advances edge intelligence in resource-constrained rail transit environments by reducing dependence on cloud servers and providing a practical, physics-informed, training-free solution for enhancing data integrity in predictive maintenance and safety-critical applications. The approach is particularly significant for real-Time onboard diagnostics where low latency and high-frequency signal preservation are essential for early fault detection such as wheel flats and bearing wear.  © 2026 IEEE.
