AD  - University of Doha for Science and Technology, College of Computing and Information Technology, Doha, Qatar
AB  - Constrained Internet of Things (IoT) endpoints must ensure high security under strict energy, memory, and hardware (HW) budgets. However, the reported implementation results for lightweight cryptography (LWC) remain difficult to compare and reuse because they span heterogeneous platforms, technology nodes, toolchains, and measurement methodologies, hindering fair cross-study comparisons and limiting reproducibility. This survey provides an implementation-oriented synthesis and derives a constraint-aware selection guide grounded in representative device envelopes, using the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) RFC 7228 Class 0/1/2 taxonomy as a consistent reference. We consolidate evidence across microcontroller units (MCU), field-programmable gate arrays (FPGA), and application-specific integrated circuit (ASIC) studies using a unified set of performance criteria: latency (cycles and time), throughput and normalization rules for cross-platform comparability, energy when reported, and area/resource and memory footprints, together with the disclosed assumptions needed for interpretability. We introduce an optimization-driven taxonomy that links dominant bottlenecks to recurring implementation patterns (datapath and state organization, memory strategy, diffusion realization, and hardening overhead). Finally, we synthesize public-key enablement for constrained provisioning and session establishment, covering representative elliptic-curve cryptography (ECC), Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC), and hybrid profiles with an emphasis on endpoint-relevant costs such as message size, latency, and peak memory. The resulting guide maps device budgets and message/workload characteristics to practical algorithms and mode choices for constrained IoT deployment.  © 2013 IEEE.
