TI  - LUT-KAN: Look-up table acceleration of Kolmogorov–Arnold networks on resource-constrained microcontrollers for embedded edge inference
PY  - 2026
T2  - Journal of Systems Architecture
VL  - 179
C7  - 103923
DO  - 10.1016/j.sysarc.2026.103923
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045094466?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Theoretical and Applied Sciences (DiSTA), eCampus University, Via Isimbardi 10, Novedrate, 22060, Italy
AD  - SMARTEST Research Center, eCampus University, Via Isimbardi 10, Novedrate, 22060, Italy
AD  - Department of Intelligent Software Systems and Technologies, School of Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence, V.N. Karazin Kharkiv National University, 4 Svobody Sq., Kharkiv, 61022, Ukraine
AB  - Kolmogorov–Arnold Networks (KANs) have emerged as a promising alternative to multilayer perceptrons, replacing fixed activation functions with learnable univariate spline-based functions on edges. However, KAN inference requires repeated evaluation of computationally expensive basis functions — such as B-splines, Jacobi polynomials, or Chebyshev polynomials — which severely limits deployment on resource-constrained microcontrollers (MCUs) that lack hardware floating-point units. We propose LUT-KAN, a systematic framework for accelerating KAN inference on MCUs through precomputed look-up tables (LUTs) with 8-bit asymmetric quantization and fixed-point linear interpolation. Our approach replaces expensive runtime polynomial evaluation with O(1) table look-up and fixed-point interpolation, reducing per-edge floating-point operations from O(d) or O(k2) to a small constant (2–3 operations for dequantization), while performing segment indexing and interpolation entirely in integer arithmetic. We present a systematic cross-platform evaluation of LUT-based KAN acceleration across four microcontroller architectures spanning three instruction set architectures: ATmega2560 (8-bit AVR, 16 MHz), RP2040 (ARM Cortex-M0+, 133 MHz), STM32F103 (ARM Cortex-M3, 72 MHz), and ESP32-C3 (RISC-V, 160 MHz)—all without hardware FPU. Across 259 benchmark configurations encompassing Jacobi (Chebyshev) polynomials of degree 3–20 and cubic B-splines with 5–8 grid points, LUT-KAN achieves speedups of 1.0×–29.7× over floating-point baselines. B-spline basis functions yield median speedups of 14.8×–23.8× across platforms, while Jacobi polynomials exhibit a linear scaling relationship of approximately 0.4×d at degree d, reaching 6.3×–8.9× at degree 20. A fixed-point LUT kernel variant further improves median speedups by 30%–47%, reaching up to 56.2× on a trained model. An ablation study demonstrates that quantization alone (without LUT) provides no speedup (median 0.36×–0.45×), confirming that the acceleration derives entirely from the table look-up structure. Maximum absolute approximation errors remain below 0.28 for all configurations, with B-spline errors consistently below 0.11. On a trained degree-16 Chebyshev KAN for sine regression, LUT quantization introduces negligible task-level accuracy loss (ΔMSE<2×10−5). The complete open-source framework — including Python-to-C code generation, automated PlatformIO build, and Wokwi-based hardware-in-the-loop testing — enables reproducible deployment of quantized KAN models on commodity embedded hardware. To validate the framework on a realistic multi-layer deployment, we present end-to-end measurements of a three-layer B-spline KAN [78 → 32 → 16 → 1] for intrusion detection (CICIDS2017) running on physical ESP32-C3 and Arduino Mega 2560 boards: inference latency is 25.8 ms with only 18 KB runtime SRAM, and F1-score degradation remains below 0.3% across all tested configurations relative to the floating-point baseline (0.9900). Our results demonstrate that LUT-based quantization transforms KANs from compute-bound floating-point algorithms into memory-bound integer operations, enabling accelerated edge AI inference on sub-dollar microcontrollers. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
