AB  - Smart city services increasingly rely on continuous IoT data streams for real-time decision-making; however, current cloud-centric and domain-isolated blockchain deployments cannot provide verifiable cross-domain trust, leaving constrained devices dependent on unverifiable assumptions. Existing interoperability protocols require uniform cryptographic capabilities that devices in the microcontroller unit (MCU) class cannot support, whereas reputation-based consensus schemes compute trust only on local metrics and cannot operate across heterogeneous domains. Critically, most omnichain frameworks collapse economic settlement into a single logical locus, creating a Single-Point-of-Value bottleneck that prevents decentralization from scaling with the system load. PoVIChain addresses these limitations through Proof-of-Verified-Interaction (PoVI). In this scheme, validator reputation emerges solely from cryptographically proven cross-domain interactions, combined with a hybrid verification framework that guarantees constant-time proof validation on MCU devices while offloading proving tasks to gateway and server-class nodes. A Smart Zone dispatcher shards economic security by routing transactions to isolated settlement zones; this effectively decouples message routing from the concentration of value, preventing systemic failure from localized exploits. Under simulation IoT workloads, PoVIChain demonstrates robust Sybil resistance while delivering 24–32% higher throughput, 20–30% lower latency, and 20–24% lower energy consumption per transaction at the protocol level measured at the consensus and verification layers and excluding physical-layer retransmission overheads, compared to representative systems across interoperability, IoT blockchain, and reputation-driven consensus designs. These results show that verifiable, resource-aware cross-chain interactions are achievable without excluding constrained devices. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
