AB  - The ARM architecture dominates IoT, healthcare, and embedded systems, ranging from ultra-low-power CortexM microcontrollers to high-performance Cortex-A processors. Providing secure authentication on these devices is challenging because cryptographic operations must satisfy stringent energy, memory, and latency constraints. Classical mechanisms, particularly optimized elliptic-curve cryptography, offer strong security with moderate cost, while lightweight authenticated ciphers enable sub-millisecond authentication for continuous sensing applications. However, emerging quantum threats jeopardize the longterm security of classical solutions. Recent studies have therefore evaluated post-quantum schemes such as Kyber, Saber, and Dilithium on ARM platforms, demonstrating practical feasibility through DSP extensions, memory-efficient implementations, and parameter tuning. Hybrid classical-post-quantum constructions further provide transitional security with manageable overhead. This survey presents a systematic review of authentication mechanisms on ARM-based devices and introduces a unified taxonomy and quantitative comparison framework. Execution time, energy consumption, memory footprint, and security guarantees are analyzed with emphasis on resource-constrained healthcare IoT, highlighting key trade-offs.  © 2026 IEEE.
