AB  - In response to the problems of high computational cost, large memory footprint, and high-power consumption of deep learning models in the era of Edge AI, this paper designs and implements a lightweight facial recognition system based on the STM32 microcontroller (NUCLEO-F334R8). The STM32 is used as the core control center, combined with the OV7670 camera module for image acquisition. To overcome the resource constraints of embedded systems, the system utilizes a pipeline of Principal Component Analysis (PCA) for dimensionality reduction and a Linear Support Vector Machine (SVM) for classification, replacing traditional Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN). The Labeled Faces in the Wild (LFW) dataset was optimized and balanced to train the model. Experimental tests show that the system achieves a projected accuracy of 80.29% for limited person recognition, realizing the automatic identification of individuals with good real-time performance and low memory usage, which meets the design requirements of embedded security applications. © 2026 IEEE.
