AB  - As a fundamental task in natural language processing (NLP), named entity recognition (NER) supports numerous downstream applications such as information retrieval and knowledge graph (KG) construction. In recent years, the rapid development of edge computing, coupled with increasing demands for real-time text processing and data security, has driven the migration of NER tasks from cloud-based to on-device deployment. Benefiting from the powerful semantic understanding enabled by large-scale parameters, large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated substantial potential in NER tasks with the advancement of fine-tuning techniques. However, current LLMs face challenges in NER tasks under resource-constrained local environments, as their large parameter sizes hinder deployment and their fine-tuning performance remains limited. To address these issues, this paper proposes LLaMA-Bi-LSTM, a lightweight model that integrates a small-parameter LLM with a bidirectional long short-term memory (Bi-LSTM) layer. The LLM component is fine-tuned for NER using the low-rank adaptation (LoRA) approach, while the Bi-LSTM layer compensates for the language model's limited capacity to learn entity boundaries. Experimental results on the CoNLL++ dataset show that LLaMA-Bi-LSTM achieves an F1 score of 95.23%, outperforming other baseline models and improving the LLM without Bi-LSTM by 19.01%. These results confirm its suitability for NER in resource-constrained environments, enabling reduced cloud computation and real-time edge deployment.  © 2026 IEEE.
