AB  - In recent years, the deployment of Artificial Intelligence (AI) on a wide spectrum of applications has experienced significant growth, with a marked increase in interest towards integrating Deep Learning (DL) algorithms within Edge devices. An initial strategy for running neural networks on resource-constrained systems involved exchanging data with a central server performing all necessary computations to update the network weights, limiting the Edge only to the inference task. Training directly on-device remains a significant challenge, largely due to the computationally intensive nature of the Backpropagation (BP) algorithm. BP requires the storage and processing of intermediate activations and gradients during both the forward and backward passes, leading to high memory consumption and computational complexity, which often exceed the capabilities of resource-constrained hardware like Microcontroller (MCU) boards. In this paper, we propose a novel on-device training method that integrates BP with stochastic sparse weight updates to significantly reduce computational complexity, thus making it affordable for devices with limited computing power. We validated our approach on the MNIST and FMNIST datasets, demonstrating that the proposed Sparse-BP method achieves competitive performance compared to traditional BP, while dramatically reducing computational demands and training times. These results illustrate the potential for deploying more advanced DL models on resource-constrained systems, enabling real-time, adaptive learning in Edge computing environments. © 2026 IEEE.
