AB  - This study presents a novel semantic-linguistic feature decoupling framework designed to significantly enhance the performance and generalization capabilities of multilingual neural machine translation (MNMT). By employing a specialized dual-channel feedforward network, the proposed approach explicitly separates language-agnostic semantic representations from language-specific linguistic features. This structural decoupling facilitates more robust cross-lingual knowledge transfer and effectively mitigates interference between diverse languages. Furthermore, the framework is integrated with a lightweight Transformer architecture that leverages dynamic sparse attention mechanisms and Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA). This combination ensures both exceptional computational efficiency and high-quality translation outputs. Comprehensive experimental evaluations on standard benchmarks such as IWSLT2017 demonstrate remarkable gains: the model achieves a substantial BLEU score improvement of +3.74 in zero-shot translation directions. Simultaneously, it optimizes operational efficiency by reducing memory usage by 62.5% and increasing inference speed threefold. Ultimately, this framework successfully addresses the critical issue of off-target generation, offering a scalable solution suitable for deployment in diverse resource-constrained environments. © 2026 IEEE.
