TI  - A survey of parameter-efficient fine-tuning techniques for audio foundation models
PY  - 2026
T2  - International Journal of Speech Technology
VL  - 29
IS  - 3
C7  - 59
DO  - 10.1007/s10772-025-10245-6
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044708230?origin=resultslist
AD  - Seattle, 98109, WA, United States
AB  - Deep learning has revolutionized audio processing, with foundation models like Wav2Vec 2.0 (317 M parameters), HuBERT, and Whisper achieving state-of-the-art results across speech recognition, speaker verification, and sound event detection. However, fine-tuning these massive models for domain-specific tasks is computationally prohibitive, requiring updating hundreds of millions of parameters with bytes of memory overhead (where is parameter count) and substantial GPU/TPU resources. Parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) techniques address this limitation by updating only a small subset of model parameters while preserving or enhancing performance. This survey systematically reviews PEFT methods that update only 0.1–5% of model parameters while achieving good performance across diverse audio tasks. Across the audio tasks surveyed, these methods recover substantial fractions of full fine-tuning performance: adapter-based methods achieve comparable word error rates to full fine-tuning on domain-specific ASR (Thomas et al., 2022) LoRA recovers competitive performance on speech emotion recognition with fewer than 0.5% trainable parameters (Feng & Narayanan, 2023), and prefix tuning enables effective few-shot adaptation in speaker verification (Sang et al., 2025). This is the first survey to comprehensively examine PEFT techniques specifically for audio foundation models. Unlike existing surveys focusing on NLP domains or general transfer learning, this work uniquely addresses audio-specific challenges. Our key contributions include: (1) the first comprehensive taxonomy categorizing PEFT methods into adapter-based, prompt-based, low-rank, and sparse approaches with formal mathematical definitions adapted to audio architectures; (2) extensive quantitative analysis comparing trainable parameters, memory usage, training time, and task-specific performance across methods; (3) evidence-based practical guidelines for method selection considering task requirements, resource constraints, and deployment scenarios from cloud to edge devices; and (4) identification of critical gaps including absent standardized benchmarking protocols that hinder cross-method comparison, limited exploration of multimodal PEFT for audio-vision and audio-text tasks, insufficient robustness evaluation under domain shift and adversarial conditions, and under-explored hybrid strategies combining multiple PEFT methods despite potential synergies. (He et al., 2022; Sang et al., 2025). We outline future research directions emphasizing unified evaluation frameworks, cross-modal transfer learning, hybrid PEFT architectures, and real-world generalization to diverse acoustic conditions. This survey establishes a foundation for systematic advancement of parameter-efficient fine-tuning in audio foundation models, enabling deployment of powerful audio AI systems in resource-constrained environments. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2026.
