AB  - Emergency communications play a critical role in disaster scenarios characterized by power outages, network disruptions, and transportation failures. Under these constraints, designing efficient security mechanisms becomes particularly challenging, as IoT devices typically operate with limited computational resources, strict energy budgets, and stringent real-time requirements. To meet the demands for rapid response, low power consumption, and high reliability, this study constructs a self-organizing MESH-based emergency communication testbed using the STM32F103 microcontroller and TPUNB technology. On this platform, we implement and compare the software and hardware performance of AES, SPECK, and the lightweight cipher RECTANGLE. Experimental results show that RECTANGLE outperforms AES and SPECK in encryption latency, Flash usage, and RAM consumption, making it more suitable for resource-constrained emergency communication terminals. End-to-end validation further demonstrates that RECTANGLE maintains ciphertext-plaintext consistency, while the overall system achieves an average latency of 527 ms and near-zero packet loss in outdoor scenarios. These findings confirm the feasibility and effectiveness of the proposed design and offer insights into deploying lightweight cryptography in emergency communication systems, contributing a practical reference for building autonomous, secure, and reliable IoT-based emergency communication infrastructures. © 2026 IEEE.
