AB  - Precision Livestock Farming on open pasture relies on continuous behaviour monitoring through worn trackers, but this requires compact models that run on IoT devices with battery and memory limits. A search over architectures and inputs found that pairing the body-parallel accelerometer axis with Overall Dynamic Body Acceleration (ODBA) in a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) network achieves 76.40% pooled accuracy (80.48% after k = 10 voting) over six behaviour classes under leave-one-animal-out cross-validation. In the 64-unit input sweep, this pairing outperformed a three-axis + ODBA counterpart by around 10 percentage points on raw accelerometer output. However, that advantage does not survive per-channel standardisation or carry to a second, collar-mounted dataset, so we report it as specific to this configuration rather than a general result. Engineered jerk channels and tuning, selected on the evaluation folds, raised accuracy to 81.92% at 23.4 kB (against 75.92% in the same run), or 84.78% without class rebalancing at a reduced walking recall (we recommend the class-balanced variant). The 32-unit reference model occupies 22.4 kB of weights and runs in 140.7 ms on a 64 MHz ARM microcontroller; the smallest variant tested, an 8-unit model, quantises to a complete 1.35 kB INT8 image running in 30.4 ms. © 2014 IEEE.
