AB  - In-network caching is a promising approach to aid multi-hop Wireless Sensor Networks (WSNs), where intermediate nodes not only forward data but also store it for later requests. This paper presents a baseline, hardware-based experimental evaluation of the benefits of in-network caching in a multi-hop network consisting of constrained microcontroller-class devices on a hardware testbed based on the IEEE 802.15.4 radio technology. A scenario of a fixed-size file delivery from a designated source to a destination node across multiple relay nodes that cache and forward content is employed for baseline establishment. We focus on practical feasibility, reproducibility, and end-toend service performance. With a probabilistic caching strategy and operating under limited memory constraints, we find that end-to-end service performance (as total transmission time) is improved by at least 10% and the number of transmissions within the network required to transmit the entire file is reduced by at least 20%. These findings establish a realistic performance baseline for in-network caching on constrained Internet of Things (IoT) hardware and highlight its potential as a low-complexity optimization for industrial multi-hop WSN deployments. We outline the potential for future research avenues based on the need for lightweight caching mechanisms in improving communication overhead in resource-constrained IoT networks. © 2026 IEEE.
