AB  - Wireless sensor networks (WSNs) support applications ranging from healthcare monitoring to environmental sensing and tactical surveillance. These systems typically incorporate low-power microcontrollers with tight constraints on energy, memory, and computation, yet they must process sizable data streams that demand strong security mechanisms. This paper presents an efficient implementation of elliptic curve cryptography (ECC) for the MSP430, a widely used ultra-low-power processor for WSN motes. Our approach integrates Edwards curve point arithmetic, a fully recursive Karatsuba-Ofman method for finite-field arithmetic, and applies 4NAF and Comb optimizations to accelerate scalar multiplication, the dominant operation in ECC. On the MSP430F1611, an ultra-low-power member of the MSP430 family of microcontrollers, our implementation achieves 169-bit ECC scalar multiplication, providing roughly 84-bit security, in only 0.87 s for random points and 0.44 s for fixed points. These results match or exceed previously published performance on this platform and demonstrate the practicality of public-key cryptography on highly resource-limited embedded systems. © 2026 IEEE.
