AD  - Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, North Carolina Agricultural and Technical State University, NC, United States
AD  - Department of Computer Science and Electrical Engineering, University of Maryland Baltimore County, MD, United States
AB  - With the rapid expansion of Internet of Things (IoT) deployments across critical infrastructure and consumer domains, ensuring the trustworthiness of resource-constrained devices is challenging. Remote attestation verifies software integrity by generating and validating a cryptographic digest of firmware or application memory image; however, secure digest generation typically relies on Trusted Execution Environments (TEEs), Memory Protection Units (MPUs), or dedicated hardware modules often unavailable in low-cost microcontrollers. This work advocates native support for remote attestation at the instruction set architecture (ISA) level by introducing custom privileged instructions for software measurement and digest obfuscation anchored to a hardware root of trust. By embedding attestation primitives directly into the processor pipeline, atomicity, privilege control, and interrupt behavior are defined architecturally rather than enforced through firmware. The proposed methodology is applied to a RISC-V processor where custom ISA extensions are developed and evaluated on an FPGA, analyzing instruction latency and overall attestation overhead. Experimental results show that the ISA extension adds only modest hardware overhead, increasing LUT and flip-flop utilization by 16.5% and 17.1%, respectively, over the baseline Murax SoC. The validation further confirms hardware-enforced privilege isolation and attack resilience through interrupt-aware abort and safe re-execution, preventing unauthorized or partial digest commitment. © 2026
