AB  - Road surface classification is a fundamental capability for smart mobility, infrastructure monitoring, and pervasive sensing systems. This paper investigates the end-to-end deployment of classical Machine Learning (ML) algorithms on an ESP32-S3 microcontroller for real-time road surface classification. Using a balanced dataset of 2,721 samples collected across six bicycle and automotive road-surface conditions, we convert each model into optimized C code and evaluate both classification performance and embedded deployment metrics. Our results characterize the trade-offs between accuracy, memory footprint, and per-sample inference time, providing a comparative assessment of ML algorithms under strict resource constraints. The findings highlight the practical viability of classical ML at the extreme edge and offer guidance for the design of low-cost, infrastructure-independent road-surface sensing systems in pervasive computing environments.  © 2026 IEEE.
