AD  - Computing for Sustainability and Social Good (C2SG) Research Group, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, United International University, Dhaka, Bangladesh
AD  - Department of Computer Science and Engineering, BRAC University, Dhaka, Bangladesh
AB  - Personalized health monitoring, including Human Activity Recognition (HAR) and Fall Detection (FD), is critical for healthcare. However, traditional cloud-dependent processing introduces latency, privacy risks, and high energy consumption, which are major limitations for real-time, battery-powered applications. Hence, this study addresses the issues utilizing on-device data processing leveraging cost-effective microcontrollers. We used the UCI HAPT and UMAFall Detection datasets to optimize Decision Tree and Random Forest models for deployment on Arduino UNO and ESP32. These devices offer significant cost and power efficiency advantages for edge computing. We also explored several optimization strategies, including discretization, which is an underexplored topic. Using a subject-dependent validation approach, our models achieved up to 99% accuracy and F1 score on standard hardware. Deployed models retained high fidelity, achieving 96.2%–98.8% accuracy on the HAPT dataset with inference latencies as low as 58 μs on ESP32 and 85 μs on UNO. Similarly, for UMAFall, we observed 95.3%–96.8% accuracy with low latencies of 45 μs and 88 μs, respectively. Furthermore, we proposed an end-to-end prediction system on the ESP32, including raw data preprocessing, which delivers predictions within 520 μs and 1228 μs for the UMAFall and HAPT datasets, respectively. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
