UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046312527?origin=resultslist
AD  - Zhejiang University, School of Computer Science and Technology, Zhejiang, Hangzhou, 310000, China
AD  - China Southern Power Grid (CSG), Electric Power Research Institute, Guangdong Provincial Key Laboratory of Power System Network Security, Guangdong, Guangzhou, 510663, China
AB  - The number theoretic transform (NTT) is a core building block of lattice-based postquantum cryptography (PQC) and a major target for soft analytical side-channel attacks (SASCAs), especially in Internet of Things (IoT) deployments where module-lattice-based key-encapsulation mechanism (ML-KEM) runs on resource-constrained microcontrollers with stable physical leakage and tight performance and memory budgets. Existing SASCA attacks against ML-KEM usually incur high computational cost and depend heavily on leakage from the inputs to the first NTT layer. To address these limitations, we propose GRALE, a practical SASCA scheme for single-trace attacks on the NTT during ML-KEM key generation. GRALE combines graph-based reduction (GR) and auxiliary leakage exploitation (ALE). GR removes the first few NTT layers from the factor graph by exploiting the invertibility of their values under ML-KEM’s small-secret distribution, reducing graph size and message-passing cost. ALE further exploits auxiliary leakage from more informative side-channel observations in subsequent polynomial multiplications in the NTT domain, reducing reliance on first-layer input leakage. Under a hamming-weight leakage model with additive Gaussian noise and using measurements from a Cortex-M4 microcontroller, GRALE achieves full key recovery when noise satisfies \sigma \lt 0.6, reduces end-to-end attack time by up to 50% compared with state-of-the-art baselines, and gains a further 30% improvement when ALE is combined with GR. © 2014 IEEE.
