DO  - 10.1109/ICDCECE68961.2026.11645838
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048469346?origin=resultslist
AD  - Ramaiah Institute of Technology, Department of Cse (AI & ML), Bengaluru, India
AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) achieve state-of-the-art performance on natural language processing tasks but impose substantial computational demands that limit accessibility in academic and resource-constrained environments. This paper presents a comprehensive empirical study comparing three fine-tuning strategies - 4-bit QLoRA, 16-bit LoRA, and 16-bit Full Fine-Tuning - applied to Facebook's OPT-1.3B on the IMDb sentiment classification benchmark. Experiments are conducted with 2,500 training and 2,500 test samples, class-wise evaluation metrics, proper latency methodology with GPU synchronization (torch.cuda.synchronize), and actual power measurement via nvidia-smi. A LoRA rank ablation study (r ∈{4,8,16}) further characterises the accuracy-efficiency trade-off under 4-bit quantization. Results show that 4-bit QLoRA achieves competitive accuracy with only 0.32% trainable parameters, reducing peak GPU memory by ≈ 75% relative to 16-bit Full Fine-Tuning while incurring a negligible accuracy penalty. The study provides reproducible, hardware-aware insights for deploying parameter-efficient LLMs on consumer-grade GPUs.  © 2026 IEEE.
