SP  - 840
EP  - 844
DO  - 10.1109/MWSCAS67364.2026.11680916
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105051199030?origin=resultslist
AD  - Michigan State University, Department Of Electrical And Computer Engineering, East Lansing, 48824, MI, United States
AB  - Real-time speaker verification (SV) on resource-constrained edge devices (smartwatches, hearables, and standalone meeting assistants) is attractive for privacy and responsiveness, yet the field's shift toward large deep-learning (DL) speaker embeddings such as x-vectors and ECAPA-TDNN imposes memory and latency costs that are prohibitive for ultra-low-power microcontroller units (MCUs). Rather than contesting cloud-scale accuracy, the question addressed here is what verification quality is reachable under strict MCU limits. This work presents a profiling and analysis framework that evaluates 240 feature-classifier pipelines, comparing classical digital signal processing (DSP) features paired with lightweight classifiers and modern DL embeddings under a single-core, memory-bounded edge simulation. Each pipeline is scored for accuracy by its equal error rate (EER), latency by its real-time factor (RTF), and peak memory. Selection is posed first as a hard constraint-satisfaction problem (CSP) and then as a tunable multi-objective figure of merit (FOM) optimization problem. Although DL embeddings dominate raw accuracy, every embedding pipeline overruns the 256 KB budget, leaving 112 feasible DSP pipelines. Among these, a logistic-regression classifier on pooled mel-frequency cepstral coefficients (MFCCs) is the most preference-robust choice for 256 KB-class devices, at 1 6. 6 % EER, 44 KB, and an estimated on-device RTF of 0.28. Personalizing per speaker further reduces mean EER to 5.95%. © 2026 IEEE.
