AD  - Nanjing University of Posts and Telecommunication, College of Internet of Things, Nanjing, China
AD  - Temple University, Center for Networked Computing, Philadelphia, United States
AB  - Transformer models have become the foundation of modern artificial intelligence, particularly in large language models (LLMs). They have achieved strong performance in natural language processing, computer vision, and multimodal learning. However, fine-tuning these models on edge devices is hindered by high memory and computational demands. Parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) methods such as BitFit, Adapter, and LoRA can reduce training cost. Nevertheless, they still rely on intermediate activations and backpropagation, which continues to cause high memory usage. Memory-efficient fine-tuning (MEFT) approaches attempt to mitigate this issue by avoiding full backpropagation or introducing side networks. Yet existing methods like LST, E3VA, and PAC either suffer from excessive structural complexity or fail to achieve effective feature fusion. To address these limitations, we propose Grouped Dual Parallel Scalable Adapters (G-DPSA). G-DPSA decouples adapters from the backbone through parallel side networks and integrates lateral and vertical adapters to capture both local and cross-layer features. In addition, a backbone grouping strategy reduces redundant modules and unnecessary computations. Experiments show that G-DPSA can improve memory efficiency by 6.07 × compared with full fine-tuning while maintaining similar accuracy. Compared with LST, E3VA, and PAC, G-DPSA can further enhance both memory efficiency and accuracy. These improvements indicate strong practicality in resource-constrained edge scenarios. © 2025 IEEE.
