AB  - The Constrained Application Protocol (CoAP) has emerged as a lightweight communication standard for constrained IoT devices, but its simplicity leaves systems vulnerable to resource exhaustion attacks that can severely degrade performance or cause denial of service. Traditional intrusion detection methods are either too resource-intensive or lack accuracy when deployed on constrained environments. This thesis proposes a lightweight detection framework that combines byte-level sequence modeling with self-supervised pretraining, knowledge distillation, and quantization-aware training. Raw CoAP traffic is tokenized at the byte level, embedded into fixed-length windows, and modeled using an LSTM/TCN-based architecture. A compact Transformer teacher model is first pretrained with masked-byte modeling to capture latent protocol semantics, and its knowledge is distilled into lightweight student models optimized for constrained devices. Quantization-aware training further compresses the models into efficient 8-bit representations while maintaining accuracy. Experimental evaluation demonstrates that the proposed framework achieves up to 99.4 % accuracy, 99.2 % precision, 99.3 % recall, F1-score of 99.3 %, and an AUC of 0.997, while reducing memory footprint by over 75 % and inference latency by more than half on Raspberry Pi 4B and ESP32-S3 microcontrollers. These results confirm that the proposed approach achieves an optimal balance between detection accuracy and deployment efficiency, enabling real-time CoAP intrusion detection across heterogeneous IoT platforms. © 2025 IEEE.
