UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031382513?origin=resultslist
AD  - Rv College of Engineering, Dept of ETE, Bengaluru, 560059, India
AB  - The advancement of edge computing and Tiny Machine Learning (Tiny ML) has opened new possibilities for deploying intelligent applications on resource-constrained microcontrollers. This paper presents a smart voice assistant designed for fully offline operation using Tiny ML on the ARM CortexM55 platform. Unlike traditional cloud-based voice assistants, our system ensures reduced latency, improved data privacy, and no dependence on internet connectivity. The approach involves selecting predefined voice commands, extracting Mel Frequency Cepstral Coefficients (MFCC) features, training a lightweight Neural Network model, and deploying it using the TensorFlow Lite for Microcontrollers (TFLM) framework. The model is tested using simulation tools such as Arm Virtual Hardware (AVH) and Keil Studio Cloud, eliminating the need for physical hardware during development. Experimental results show that the model achieves accuracy with minimal memory usage, making it suitable for low-power embedded devices. This study demonstrates a complete pipeline for developing and simulating voice recognition systems for edge applications.  © 2025 IEEE.
