AB  - Internet of Oceanic Things (IoOT) platforms face severe communication constraints due to limited bandwidth, intermittent connectivity, and energy restrictions in harsh maritime environments. This paper presents a lightweight compression strategy that reduces telemetry payload sizes for ZigBee-based marine deployments. The approach combines bit-width reduction, packet restructuring, and delta with run-length encoding to minimize redundant transmissions while maintaining low computational cost. Experimental evaluation demonstrated up to 56% payload reduction under idle conditions, directly lowering channel usage and improving communication efficiency. Unlike dictionary-or entropy-based schemes, the proposed method requires minimal processing and memory, making it suitable for resource-constrained microcontrollers aboard autonomous surface vehicles. The results highlight how simple, low-overhead optimizations can enhance telemetry robustness and extend system lifetime in real-world IoOT deployments, while providing a foundation for future studies on compression-energy trade-offs and secure integration with existing ZigBee protocols. © 2025 IEEE.
