AB  - Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) techniques such as Gradient ×Input (GxI), SHAP, and LIME can improve the interpretability of intrusion detection systems (IDS), but their suitability for Internet of Things (IoT) deployments remains uncertain due to limited device resources. In this work, we examine the feasibility of integrating XAI into a hybrid IDS deployed on a heterogeneous testbed consisting of Raspberry Pi 4B/5, NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano, and ESP32 devices. Our experiments measure the runtime, memory, and latency overhead introduced by XAI on actual hardware. Our experiments evaluate the runtime, memory usage, and latency overhead introduced by XAI on real hardware. Specifically, we conduct experiments in scenarios including federated network intrusion detection and local host-based log analysis. The results provide early insights into whether XAI explanations can be generated effectively on resource-constrained IoT devices without exceeding practical performance limits.  © 2025 IEEE.
