TI  - Design and Implementation of a Two-Stage Tiny Bootloader for Flash-Constrained Microcontrollers Using RAM Execution
PY  - 2024
T2  - 2024 25th International Arab Conference on Information Technology, ACIT 2024
DO  - 10.1109/ACIT62805.2024.10877186
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/86000016657?origin=resultslist
AD  - The University of Michigan Dearborn, The Electrical and Computer Engineering Department, Dearborn, MI, United States
AB  - This paper introduces an innovative two-stage Tiny Bootloader (TBL) that primarily runs in Random Access Memory (RAM), reducing reliance on Flash memory. In contrast to conventional bootloaders, which are stored mostly in Flash, this design optimizes Flash usage, which is particularly advantageous for microcontrollers with limited Flash capacity. By relocating most of the bootloader code to RAM, the proposed approach frees up Flash memory for program data, making it highly suitable for resource-constrained embedded systems. The implementation is showcased using the STM32G031K4 microcontroller with Universal Asynchronous Receiver-Transmitter (UART) communication. This paper will go over the concept, design, implementation, and testing of the new proposed Tiny Bootloader. © 2024 IEEE.
