AD  - Telkom University, School of Electrical Engineering, Bandung, Indonesia
AD  - Telkom University, The Univ CoE Intelligent Sensing-IoT, Bandung, Indonesia
AB  - Efficient driver drowsiness detection is a critical safety application, as drowsy driving is a leading cause of road accidents. Lightweight CNNs such as MobileNetV2 have been specifically designed for mobile and embedded vision tasks, offering high accuracy with low computational cost. In this study, we conduct a comparative analysis of three INT8-quantized CNN models for drowsiness detection on a resource-constrained device (ESP32-CAM): MobileNetV2 multi-output, MobileNetV2 single-output, and EfficientNet-B0. All models were trained on the same drowsiness dataset (collected via Roboflow) and evaluated using training and validation accuracy and loss, as well as confusion-matrix metrics (precision, recall, F1-score). Our results show that the MobileNetV2 single-output model achieved 96% accuracy in FLOAT32 and 93% in INT8, offering a strong balance between performance and efficiency, making it suitable for ESP32-CAM deployment. In contrast, the multi-output MobileNetV2 reached 95% accuracy in FLOAT32 but dropped to 30% in INT8, rendering it unsuitable for constrained devices. EfficientNet-B0 outperformed both in FLOAT32 with 98% accuracy and maintained 96% in INT8, but its larger model size and memory demands exceeded ESP32-CAM limitations. The main contributions of this work are: (1) a comparative analysis of three lightweight CNNs for embedded drowsiness detection, (2) evaluation of INT8 quantization on ESP32-CAM, (3) identification of MobileNetV2 single-output as the most deployable model, and (4) practical design insights for TinyML-based driver monitoring.  © 2025 IEEE.
