AB  - Wildfires constitute a significant environmental and socioeconomic threat, increasingly exacerbated by climate change and human activity. Timely detection is critical to mitigating their devastating impact on ecosystems, infrastructure, and human life. This paper presents a proactive forest fire monitoring system built on a low-cost, energy-efficient wireless sensor network (WSN) architecture enhanced with TinyML. The system employs ESP32 microcontrollers to perform real-time fire risk classification at the network edge, leveraging lightweight machine learning models for temperature, humidity, and gas data analysis. A dynamic Cluster Head (CH) election mechanism based on signal strength and battery levels ensures balanced energy consumption across nodes. Experimental results demonstrate the effectiveness of the system in detecting early fire indicators, validating its suitability for deployment in remote, resource-constrained environments. © 2025 IEEE.
