AD  - University of Texas at Austin, Department of Computer Science, Austin, TX, United States
AD  - Polytechnic Institute, Purdue University, West Lafayette, IN, United States
AD  - University of Texas San Antonio, Department of IS and Cybersecurity, San Antonio, TX, United States
AB  - The rapid expansion of the Internet of Things (IoT) has introduced a vast, vulnerable attack surface composed of devices with strict constraints on computation, memory, and energy. Traditional host or network centric intrusion detection systems (IDS) often exceed these limitations, prompting over a decade of research into lightweight, AI driven alternatives. This review synthesizes over eighty peer reviewed studies from 2013 to 2024 that apply machine learning or deep learning techniques to IoT security. The works are grouped into three main design families: (i) anomaly detection models using shallow learners or autoencoders, (ii) tree based and other classical classifiers optimized via feature selection or heuristic tuning, and (iii) distributed and federated approaches that shift heavy computation to edge or fog layers. Models are evaluated on detection accuracy, false-alarm rate, CPU overhead, memory footprint, and energy use. Results show that compressed deep learning and hybrid ensembles can now exceed 97 % detection rates while using under 15 MB of memory and < 8% CPU on typical microcontroller platforms. However, challenges remain such as scarce labeled IoT attack data, concept drift, and lack of standardized evaluation frameworks across diverse hardware. The review concludes by highlighting future directions, including adaptive continual learning, privacy-preserving model updates, and lightweight IDS benchmarks, to help guide the development of deployable, resource efficient intrusion detection for emerging IoT systems.  © 2025 IEEE.
