AB  - This study investigates fine-tuning LLaMA 3.1 on a custom dataset of a clinical consensus document with the goal of enhancing clinical guideline adherence using efficient AI-driven solutions. Three optimization techniques - Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), Quantized LoRA (QLoRA), and Bias-terms Fine-tuning (BitFit) - are explored for adapting large language models while maintaining computational feasibility. We evaluate each fine-tuned model's computational efficiency (memory usage, training time) and clinical accuracy using a dataset derived from a European Society of Cardiology (ESC) consensus article, representing specific medical expert knowledge. To support real-world application, we developed an interactive Streamlit-based interface, allowing users to query the models and generate interpretive responses from three locally stored, fine-tuned models, ensuring privacy-preserving, AI-assisted recommendations. A pediatric expert benchmarks the models by formulating questions and extracting reference answers from the ESC consensus knowledge. The models' responses were rated based on relevance and accuracy. Additionally, we assess model performance using Translation Edit Rate (TER) and BERT-based similarity metrics. Experimental results indicate that BitFit offers the best balance between model accuracy and computational efficiency, both during training and in qualitative and quantitative evaluations. These findings suggest that BitFit provides the most effective solution for AI-driven clinical support in a resource constrained-environment, highlighting the potential of efficient fine-tuning to enhance clinical guideline adherence. Open-Source Code: https://github.com/yaseen28/hypert-ai  © 2025 IEEE.
