AD  - National Taichung University of Education, Department of Computer Science, Taichung, Taiwan
AB  - Traditional federated fine-tuning of large language models (LLMs) requires homogeneous models. Clients with limited resources may be unable to participate in federated fine-tuning due to memory constraints. To address these limitations, we introduce model pruning focused on the MLP module in LLMs prior to federated learning. Additionally, we adopt LoRA-based parameter-efficient fine-tuning to reduce the communication cost. Specifically, LoRA is applied only to the self-attention modules within LLMs. This approach separates model functionality: self-attention modules for LoRA fine-tuning and MLP modules for pruning. It enables clients to use different MLP pruning ratios without affecting the self-attention modules where LoRA is applied. Consequently, we develop an architecture using the FlowerTune framework that enables federated fine-tuning of MLP-pruned heterogeneous LLMs, allowing each client to use an LLM with a pruning ratio suitable for its hardware capabilities. Experimental results show that, when compared to homogeneous 25% uniformly pruned models, our heterogeneous approach outperforms by 9.35 and 29.05 percentage points in SQuAD and ToxiGen tasks, respectively. These results demonstrate that our approach achieves strong performance for pruned LLMs while enabling clients with limited memory resources to participate in federated fine-tuning. Overall, our work demonstrates the viability and effectiveness of federated fine-tuning across heterogeneous MLP-pruned LLMs in real-world resource-constrained environments. © 2025 IEEE.
