AB  - This paper focuses on the challenge of deploying a neural network to be used for precise localization capabilities on the constrained hardware by exploring a novel strategy, transforming raw magnetic field sensor readings into accurate real-time magnet position estimations within a defined workspace. The collected dataset of magnetic field readings, corresponding to known magnet position, was used to train different configurations of feed forward neural networks. These configurations vary in key architectural parameters, including the number of hidden layers and the number of neurons per layer. The trained networks are evaluated for their total memory footprint and execution time on an 8-bit microcontroller. This methodology, demonstrated through the localization problem, is applicable to other machine learning application, including data classification and can be adapted for different hardware constrained devices.  © 2025 IEEE.
