AB  - Human Activity Recognition (HAR) is a cornerstone technology for applications in smart homes, health monitoring, and interactive systems. While vision-based methods offer high performance, they introduce significant privacy concerns. Millimeter-wave (mmWave) radar sensors provide a privacy-preserving alternative. However, their sparse and noisy data present a performance challenge, and the complexity of deep learning models hinders their deployment on resource-constrained Microcontroller Units (MCUs). This paper introduces a comprehensive framework to address these interconnected challenges. We propose a lightweight Spatial-Temporal Graph Convolutional Network (ST-GCN) for mmWave point cloud data, enhanced through a novel cross-modal knowledge distillation methodology. In our framework, a high-performance teacher model, trained on rich skeleton joint data, guides the training of a compact student model that operates solely on mmWave data during inference. This approach is augmented by a multi-faceted lightweighting strategy, including architectural tuning, post-training quantization, and a principled removal of residual connections, which drastically reduces the peak memory footprint to meet MCU constraints. The robustness and generalizability of our framework are validated through extensive experiments on two distinct datasets. On the DGUHA dataset, our method improves accuracy by up to 7.46%. Critically, on the large-scale and complex MilliPoint benchmark, our framework demonstrates consistent performance gains, improving accuracy by up to 7.89%. The final, optimized model is successfully deployed on a commercial MCU, showing its practical viability for real-time, accurate, and privacy-conscious HAR on edge devices. © 2013 IEEE.
