AB  - The explosion of IoT edge devices has made data security, authenticity, and integrity increasingly difficult to guarantee in settings with limited resources. Low-power Internet of Things (IoT) nodes find modern cryptographic methods computationally demanding and challenging. This work proposes and uses a Secure Hashing Algorithm (SHA-LWC) customized to Internet of Things (IoT) edge devices. Thanks to the suggested algorithm's balance of cryptographic strength and low resource consumption, secure data processing is accomplished with the least effect on device performance or energy economy. SHA-LWC maximizes the 8-bit and 16-bit microcontrollers usually found in edge devices using simplified substitution-permutation networks and limited-round operations. The results reveal that security analysis is strong against common attacks, including differential cryptanalysis, preimage, and collision. Among other criteria, the method runs faster in processing time, memory footprint, and energy consumption than currently available lightweight hash functions, including SPONGent, PHOTON, and Quark. Results of tests on the Raspberry Pi and Arduino platforms show the practicality of the algorithm in many Internet of Things (IoT) environments, including smart home automation, health monitoring, and environmental sensing. SHA-LWC is a valuable technique for safeguarding data at the periphery of the Internet of Things (IoT) ecosystem due to its security aspects and efficiency. This work provides the framework for scalable and safe edge computing, which is in demand in the fast-expanding IoT industry.  © 2025 IEEE.
