AD  - IIT Patna, India
AD  - BITS Pilani, United Arab Emirates
AB  - Deep learning has revolutionized numerous domains such as computer vision, natural language processing, and speech recognition, achieving human-level performance in many tasks. However, deploying deep neural networks (DNNs) on resource-constrained embedded systems remains a critical challenge due to stringent limitations in flash storage, SRAM, and compute capabilities. This work presents an adaptive post-training quantization (PTQ) framework tailored for device-aware TinyML deployment across diverse microcontroller architectures. Our approach performs fine-grained layer-wise quantization decisions by profiling the sensitivity of individual network layers to precision reduction and enabling mixed-precision quantization that strategically applies INT8 and INT4 weight representations. The method leverages per-channel quantization for convolutional layers and employs BatchNorm folding to preserve model accuracy without retraining. We systematically evaluate the framework on multiple representative microcontroller platforms (e.g., ARM Cortex-M4 and Cortex-M7), demonstrating up to 65% model size reduction and 45% inference latency improvement with less than a 10% accuracy degradation on standard benchmarks such as CIFAR-10 and ImageNet subsets. The proposed solution bridges the gap between high-performance DNNs and stringent hardware constraints, providing a scalable and automated pipeline for deploying adaptive quantized models in production-grade TinyML applications.  © 2025 IEEE.
