AD  - Integrated Circuit and System (i-CAS) Lab, Department of ECE, National Institute of Technology, Arunachal Pradesh, 791113, India
AB  - Secure and power-efficient access control is a critical requirement in modern mechatronic, embedded, and IoT systems. Conventional solutions based on microcontrollers (MCUs), RFID, or FPGA prototypes incur significant overhead in power, area, and firmware security, limiting their applicability in resource-constrained environments. This paper presents HexLock, a 4-bit hexadecimal locking mechanism using UMC 65 nm CMOS, designed as a lightweight, ASIC-based alternative for hardware-level authentication. The proposed architecture employs a register bank for secure key storage, XOR-OR comparator logic for verification, and a false-attempt counter to resist brute-force attacks. To minimize dynamic and leakage power, optimized power gating strategies were explored and integrated, ensuring continuous operation in always-on environments. Extensive transient simulations at a supply voltage of 1.1 Volt demonstrate an average delay of 2.69 ns, maximum operating frequency of 285 MHz, at an average power consumption of approximately 76.9 µW. The results establish HexLock as a scalable, energy-efficient, and tamper-resistant security primitive suitable for smart locks, and embedded mechatronic applications. © 2025 IEEE.
