AB  - Brain-machine interfaces (BMIs) offer a promising solution for restoring motor function in individuals with severe disabilities such as tetraplegia. Although artificial neural network (ANN) decoders have achieved high decoding accuracy, their large memory footprint and high computational demands limit their applicability in implantable or embedded BMI systems. Spiking neural networks (SNNs), inspired by biological neurons, provide a more efficient alternative while maintaining comparable performance. In this study, we proposed an optimized recurrent spiking neural network (RSNN) decoder for real-time neural signal decoding and deploy it on a resource-constrained ESP32-based microcontroller unit (MCU). The proposed RSNN decoder achieved improvement of 17.92 % in coefficient of determination (R2), 7. 9 8 % in correlation coefficient (CC), and 2 5. 4 5 % in root mean squared error (RMSE) compared to a baseline ANN decoder. It also delivered a 15.96 × speed-up while having 5.47 % less memory footprint on the same MCU platform. These results demonstrate the potential of optimized RSNN for enabling energy-efficient, real-time BMI decoding on low-power embedded hardware. © 2025 IEEE.
