AB  - This study investigates the creation of a deep neural network-based speech emotion recognition (SER) model and its transformation into a TinyML model. In order to learn from sequences and perform well on SER tasks, the Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) architecture and Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients (MFCCs) are used. Additionally, TinyML is used to make machine learning models operable on resource-constrained hardware, such as microcontrollers, providing benefits for a variety of applications, including mobile, IoT, and wearable technology. The circumplex model in affective computing, which represents a two-dimensional model indicating emotions with respect to of valence and arousal, is taken into consideration in this application. Based on this framework and the resulting scenarios, the categories "Fear"and "Disgusted"are eliminated, and "Angry"and "Sad"emotions are combined into one category, "Unpleasant,"because they share similar valence and arousal characteristics. The classification of four emotions - happy, surprised, neutral, and unpleasant - is consequently a focus. Three datasets were combined to create the dataset used is a combination of three datasets, RAVDESS [21], TESS [22], and SAVEE [23]. Before exploring and displaying wave plots as well as spectrograms for various emotions, the data is imported and preprocessed. The dataset is expanded, and the model's performance is improved, using data augmentation techniques like noise addition, stretching, and pitching. After completing the typical training and validation pipeline, the model is quantized to reduce its size and converted into a TensorFlow Lite (TFLite) format. The inference time of the TFLite model is then compared to the inference time of the original model. © 2023 IEEE.
