AB  - Cognitive load assessment in real-time applications presents significant challenges for portable brain-computer interfaces and clinical monitoring systems. This work presents an edge computing approach for functional near-infrared spectroscopy (fNIRS) that integrates signal processing and deep learning inference directly on microcontroller hardware. Five time-series-to-image transformation methods were compared, with Gramian Angular Field (GAF) showing favorable performance in terms of computational efficiency and quantization stability. A CNNLSTM architecture incorporating attention mechanisms was developed to classify cognitive states across rest, 1-back, 2-back, and 3-back working memory tasks, achieving 92.28 % accuracy. Model compression through INT8 quantization reduced memory requirements by 68.08 % while maintaining 89.51 % classification performance. The implemented 8-channel fNIRS system operates at 735 nm and 850 nm wavelengths for hemodynamic monitoring, with inference latency of 78.68 ms on an ESP32S3 microcontroller. The results demonstrate the feasibility of deploying attention-based neural networks on resource-constrained embedded platforms for cognitive monitoring applications. © 2025 IEEE.
