TI  - PV Arc Fault Circuit Interrupter with Knowledge Distillation-Based Lightweight Convolutional Neural Network and SSCB Integration
PY  - 2025
T2  - IEEE Transactions on Power Electronics
VL  - 40
IS  - 12
SP  - 18189
EP  - 18201
DO  - 10.1109/TPEL.2025.3588792
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105011198636?origin=resultslist
AD  - University of North Carolina at Charlotte, Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Charlotte, 28223, NC, United States
AD  - University of North Carolina at Charlotte, Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, Charlotte, 28223, NC, United States
AB  - DC arc faults in photovoltaic systems pose serious safety risks, including fire hazards and power disruption, making accurate and timely detection essential. This article presents PArcNet, a lightweight convolutional neural network architecture optimized through knowledge distillation for efficient arc fault detection in resource-constrained environments. Trained using a high-capacity teacher model, PArcNet achieves over 98% detection accuracy with only 5.04 k parameters and a memory footprint of 90 kB—making it suitable for real-time deployment on low-cost microcontrollers. The model outperforms traditional machine learning classifiers, such as support vector machines and LightGBM in both accuracy and model efficiency, and its robustness has been confirmed through 5-fold cross-validation. For real-time validation, PArcNet was implemented on an STM32H743ZI2 microcontroller and integrated with a solid-state circuit breaker, achieving arc detection and interruption in as little as 19 ms. To further reduce false positives during transient disturbances, a 5-cycle confirmation strategy was adopted, resulting in a total response time of 95 ms. Extensive testing under varied PV operating scenarios—including inverter startup, shadow occlusion, and load fluctuations—demonstrates the model’s resilience and reliability. PArcNet offers a scalable, accurate, and fast-response solution for embedded PV arc fault protection, contributing to safer and more robust renewable energy systems. © 1986-2012 IEEE.
