AD  - Defence Institute of Advanced Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Pune, India
AB  - The rapid growth of Edge AI has fueled increasing interest in enabling neural network training directly on edge devices such as resource-constrained microcontrollers. The study in this paper addresses on-device training which offers critical advantages including enhanced data privacy, reduced latency, and improved energy efficiency, making it a cornerstone of edge intelligence in applications like healthcare monitoring, autonomous systems, and predictive maintenance. In contrast to cloud-based solutions, training at the edge must function within strict limits on memory, compute power, and energy consumption, while relying solely on locally stored data. The primary challenge lies in executing the training process within these tight resource constraints. At the same time, the computational workload of each training step must be minimized to achieve real-time responsiveness and energy efficiency. Moreover, training algorithms must ensure reliable convergence despite limited hardware capabilities and the challenges of deploying complex neural models on constrained devices. In this paper, the methodologies are proposed that addresses these challenges and presents strategies for efficient and stable neural network training on microcontroller-class edge devices, enabling adaptive and personalized intelligence at the extreme edge. The results demonstrate the efficacy of the proposed methodologies. © 2025 IEEE.
