DO  - 10.1109/ICCR67387.2025.11292602
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031591041?origin=resultslist
AD  - Universiti Tenaga Nasional (UNITEN), College of Computing and Informatics (CCI), Department of Computing, Kajang, Malaysia
AD  - United Arab Emirates University, College of It, Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates
AB  - Security of personal belongings has long been a fundamental concern, and traditional mechanical locks are vulnerable to key loss, duplication, and unauthorized access. To address these challenges, this paper presents a secure and efficient smart locker system that replaces physical keys with fingerprint-based biometric authentication. The system is implemented on an Arduino Nano microcontroller and integrates a fingerprint sensor for identity verification. During enrollment, a user's fingerprint is captured, its features extracted, and the template encrypted using a lightweight XOR-based cipher with a dynamic key derived from the user's ID and a random seed. The encrypted template is stored locally in EEPROM. During authentication, the user's fingerprint and ID are provided again, the XOR key is regenerated, and the stored template is decrypted for matching. Upon a successful match, a relay triggers the solenoid lock to grant access. Experimental results demonstrate real-time authentication with a total processing time of less than 1 second, including feature extraction ( 710 ms), matching ( 1.89 ms), and key generation (0.004 ms), with an energy cost of approximately 0.24 J per operation. Compared to recent biometric locker solutions, the proposed system offers enhanced template security, low energy consumption, and offline operation.  © 2025 IEEE.
