TI  - An Autoencoder-Based TinyML Model for On-Device Arrhythmia Detection
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 7th International Conference on Smart Applications, Communications and Networking, SmartNets 2025
DO  - 10.1109/SmartNets65254.2025.11106907
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105015501444?origin=resultslist
AD  - International Institute of Information Technology, Bangalore, India
AB  - Our work presents a method for ECG segmentation and arrhythmia detection using a tiny, autoencoder-based machine learning (ML) model for real-time on-device inference on resource-constrained wearable devices. Existing approaches rely on the frequent wireless transmission of ECG data from wearable devices to hubs or cloud servers, where they are post-processed using ML algorithms to detect arrhythmias. These approaches increases the device's energy consumption, exposes sensitive health information to eavesdropping, and delays diagnosis. However, running traditional ML models for arrhythmia detection directly on wearables supporting microcontroller units (MCUs) with limited computing power and memory is challenging. As a solution, we developed an autoencoder-based tinyML model with a limited number of layers and neurons that runs within the resource constraints of MCUs. We demonstrated the model using a simulated system that mimics a wearable performing real-time ECG segmentation and arrhythmia detection. We tested the system using multiple patient records from the MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database. The system achieved an accuracy, precision, and recall of $\mathbf{7 4. 5 8 \%, ~} \mathbf{5 8. 4 4 \%}$, and $\mathbf{9 7. 0 2 \%}$, respectively.  © 2025 IEEE.
