AB  - This paper proposes a low-code development framework for Internet of Things (IoT) applications, integrating a visual data flow programming interface with the lightweight scripting language-mruby. The aim is to reduce the complexity and technical barriers traditionally associated with embedded programming, particularly in environments with resource-constrained microcontrollers.The system enables users to design application logic visually with Node-RED, a popular data-flow implementation tool. The generated data flow is exported as a JSON file, which is then parsed by a custom mruby code generator. This generator translates the data flow into executable Ruby code, then compiles it into compact bytecode using the compiler. The resulting bytecode is then deployed to microcontrollers running a minimal mruby virtual machine (VM), enabling the efficient execution of application logic without the need for a full interpreter or operating system. To validate the system, a prototype was implemented and evaluated on an RBoard - a development board based on a PIC microcontroller with limited memory and computing resources. A simple IoT application was designed to read temperature sensor data and control an LED based on a threshold. The experimental results confirmed that the system could handle sensor input, control logic and hardware-level output in real time using only the automatically generated code.This framework significantly reduces development time and programming effort, making embedded development accessible to non-experts and facilitating rapid prototyping. Combining visual programming with mruby bytecode execution ensures ease of use and runtime efficiency. Future work will focus on extending node types, enhancing debugging and simulation capabilities, facilitating wireless deployment and expanding support for a wider range of microcontrollers. This approach demonstrates a practical path towards scalable, maintainable and user-friendly IoT development.  © 2025 IEEE.
