AB  - The Dimensionality Reduction (DR) problem serves the logic of repositioning the Edge-Fog-Cloud continuum towards extreme-edge-only logic, adopting more constrained efficient, effective, quick, and cheap computing capabilities and embracing more autonomous and self-driven technologies. In short, the paper investigates DR algorithms for machine learning in edge computing environments characterized by limited resources. We focus on their energy consumption and associated trade-offs because we recognize the critical need for resource-efficient and environmentally sustainable AI solutions. Initially, the paper's motivations are derived from the 'EcoIndex' and the 'Machine Learning for Embedded Systems' projects. Subsequently, we present our efforts to evaluate the energy consumption of DR algorithms, alongside comprehensive experiments to ascertain trade-offs. A significant contribution of this study is the development of a structured methodology that prioritizes systematic analysis over mere experimental results. Furthermore, we discuss identifying the optimal (offline and online) DR algorithm according to our predefined evaluation criteria. We also introduce a novel incremental DR algorithm and experiments dedicated to extreme-edge computing. The primary contribution of this paper is a synthesized perspective of the selected DR algorithms, allowing a discourse on the trade-offs of DR algorithms within resource-constrained environments.  © 2025 IEEE.
