UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105030537816?origin=resultslist
AD  - Graduate School of Information Technology, Siam University, Bangkok, Thailand
AB  - This work investigates the feasibility of serial-based split inference for real-time object recognition applications on resource-constrained edge devices using dual ESP32-S3 units. MobileNetV2 with pretrained ImageNet weights is employed as a feature extractor, while transfer learning is applied to retrain a lightweight classifier for six target categories. To enable efficient deployment, the model is quantized into INT8 format. The proposed system uses an ESP32-S3-CAM to capture images and generate a [1, 1280] INT8 feature vector, which is transmitted via UART to a second ESP32-S3 running a dedicated TFLite classifier. Experimental evaluation of model size, transmission latency, memory usage, and classification accuracy demonstrates the potential of this architecture for edge AI applications under strict resource constraints. While current implementation achieves 0.25 FPS, the study identifies optimization strategies required to achieve real-time performance (≥5 FPS) on microcontroller-based platforms. © 2025 IEEE.
