AB  - Wireless Sensor Networks (WSNs) are transforming smart agriculture by enabling precise monitoring and efficient resource allocation. Despite their potential, the limited energy capacity of sensor nodes poses a persistent challenge, often leading to shortened network life spans and disrupted operations. This paper presents an enhanced framework for the Distributed Energy Efficient Clustering (DEEC) protocol, specifically designed to address these challenges in agricultural IoT applications. The protocol prioritizes efficient cluster head selection by considering factors such as residual energy, proximity to base stations, and adaptability to diverse farming environments. Advanced clustering techniques, including K means and bio-inspired algorithms, are integrated into the framework to improve scalability and optimize energy use. Low power communication technologies, such as Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) and LoRa, are incorporated to ensure reliable data transmission, even in resource-constrained settings. Rigorous simulations evaluate the performance of the enhanced DEEC protocol against standard alternatives like LEACH and SEP, focusing on metrics such as energy efficiency, network lifetime, and data throughput. Results indicate that the proposed framework extends network lifetime by up to 30% and reduces energy consumption by 25%, making it particularly suited for precision farming. These findings underscore the potential of tailored WSN protocols to overcome energy limitations, providing a pathway toward more sustainable and effective smart agriculture solutions. © Grenze Scientific Society, 2025.
