AB  - This paper presents a targeted benchmarking study on model size reduction for deploying Tiny Machine Learning (TinyML) on resource-constrained Internet of Things (IoT) devices. Through systematic experiments on a convolutional neural network, we quantitatively evaluate the efficacy of post-training quantization and aggressive pruning. Our primary contribution is a novel size-based benchmark demonstrating that an aggressive pruning strategy applied to a 15.4 MB baseline model achieved a compressed size of 1.99 KB a 76.53% reduction. This result surpassed the outcomes of both Dynamic Range Quantization (2.02 KB) and Full Integer Quantization (2.58 KB), highlighting pruning's significant and often underutilized potential for minimizing the memory footprint of models destined for extreme-edge deployment. All code, models, and data for reproducing these size-reduction benchmarks are made publicly available on our GitHub repository. Furthermore, we provide a comparative analysis of leading TinyML frameworks-TensorFlow Lite Micro, Edge Impulse, and uTensor-focusing on their support for these optimization techniques and their usability. Our findings provide developers with clear, data-driven guidance on achieving maximum model compression for ARM CortexM microcontrollers and conclude with a discussion on future research directions. © 2025 IEEE.
