TI  - From Billions to Bits: A Taxonomy and Survey of Optimization Methods for Large Language Models
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 IEEE Conference on Engineering Informatics, ICEI 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICEI68356.2025.11603745
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046374031?origin=resultslist
AD  - KLE Technological University, Hubballi, India
AB  - Physician shortages, rising patient numbers, and urgent clinical judgments are putting increasing strain on health-care systems around the world, especially in settings with limited resources and little access to advanced diagnostic assistance. Large Language Models (LLMs) automate medical documentation, assisting in patient triage, and supporting diagnostic workflows, yet their deployment remains impractical: a 70 billion parameter model requires 140 GB of memory in full precision, inference latency exceeds clinical acceptability on standard hardware, and privacy regulations crutial on-premise deployment that most facilities cannot support. This survey addresses the deployment gap by analyzing optimization techniques that make LLaMA models available for real-world medical applications. We evaluate three quantization methods (GPTQ, AWQ, NF4) that compress model weights to 4-bit precision, and three parameter-efficient fine-tuning approaches (LoRA, QLoRA, AdaLoRA) that enable domain adaptation by training only 0.01-0.1% of model parameters. Experimental evaluation on WikiText-2 and MMLU benchmarks demonstrates that quantization achieves 3.5-4× memory reduction with 94-99% accuracy retention across models from 7B to 65B parameters, while PEFT methods maintain 95-99% of full fine-tuning performance. Combined optimization enables a 70B model to operate within 48 GB memory with sub-2-second inference on consumer GPUs - practical for clinical deployment. Analysis of PMC-LLaMA shows that LoRA fine-tuning improved MedQA-USMLE accuracy from 36.1% to 56.4% using only 0.036% trainable parameters. While identifying important gaps in domain-specific quantization, regulatory validation frameworks, and uncertainty quantification that need more research, our findings demonstrate that the strategic integration of quantization and parameter-efficient fine-tuning increases access to advanced medical AI by allowing resource-constrained facilities to deploy models previously only available to well-funded research institutions. © 2025 IEEE.
