AD  - Technical University of Cluj-Napoca, Applied Electronics, Romania
AB  - Modern automotive Electronic Control Units (ECUs) face increasing cybersecurity threats requiring realtime anomaly detection within severe resource constraints. Traditional intrusion detection systems rely on static thresholds, leading to high false alarm rates and missed attacks. This paper presents a novel adaptive anomaly detection approach combining unsupervised learning with reinforcement learning for resource-constrained automotive microcontrollers. The system integrates a lightweight 1D Convolutional Autoencoder (1DAE) that learns normal operational patterns from ECU sensor data with a tabular Q-learning agent that dynamically adjusts detection thresholds. The 1DAE generates reconstruction-error anomaly scores, while the Q-learning agent adapts thresholds using a safety-oriented reward function that heavily penalizes missed attacks. Experimental validation demonstrates significant improvements in detection performance, with substantially reduced false positives while maintaining high recall rates compared to static threshold approaches. The system's compact memory footprint and fast inference capabilities make it suitable for deployment on resource-constrained automotive microcontrollers under strict safety and compliance requirements.  © 2025 IEEE.
