AB  - Pretrained Transformers have enabled longhorizon, discrete-action planning, yet directly transferring them to vertical domains is hindered by scarce expert data, tight memory/compute budgets, and semantically confusable action labels. We study a generalizable adaptation workflow rather than a new monolithic model - that attaches to off-the-shelf backbones and operates under single-GPU constraints. The workflow has three stages: (i) masked next-action pretraining on inexpensive non-expert traces, (ii) semantic tokenization that factors actions into interpretable components, and (iii) specialization via lightweight Adapter/LoRA while freezing the backbone (about 2% trainable parameters). In our controlled instantiation with a compact 6-layer encoder (dmodel=768,8 heads), the method attains a favorable accuracy-efficiency trade-off on Rubik's Cube planning. While absolute accuracy slightly trails full fine-tuning, the workflow preserves portability across backbones and provides deterministic, searchfree latency - practical for data- and resource-constrained vertical settings. While our experiments use a compact 6-layer encoder under a single-GPU budget, the three-stage workflow is backbone-agnostic: it attaches via semantic tokenization and PEFT (Adapter/LoRA) to off-the-shelf pretrained Transformers without modifying their weights.  © 2025 IEEE.
