AB  - Deploying IoT systems for in-situ measurements is difficult in areas with poor telecommunications infrastructure. A viable alternative is to use Unmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV)s to collect data from end devices on the ground. However, the latter should minimize the volume of data to be transmitted via the available time window and yet use its electrical supply sparingly. To achieve this, appropriate communication channel parameters must be configured to ensure both energy efficiency and a sufficiently large transmission range, allowing the UAV to cover the maximum possible area during its mission. Various LoRa configurations were examined, focusing on transmission power, payload size, and spreading factor, to minimize energy consumption while maintaining reliable communication. To identify optimal configurations, threshold-based performance measures were incorporated into the analysis. It has been demonstrated how, based on physical properties of the measured soil signals, the data payload can be effectively reduced, and a LoRa Peer-To-Peer (P2P) method proposed for configuring communication parameters - ensuring power-efficient transmission, verified in three real-world scenarios in agriculture, forestry, and river management. The proposed solution does not require significant investments in the communications infrastructure and has great application potential in environmental monitoring. © 2025 IEEE.
