TI  - Map-Free Collective Control via RSSI-Based Quorum Sensing and On-Board Adaptation
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings - 2025 11th International Conference on Systems and Informatics, ICSAI 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICSAI68704.2025.11345852
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033345491?origin=resultslist
AD  - Universidad Distrital Francisco José de Caldas Facultad Tecnológica, D.C., Bogotá, Colombia
AB  - We present a quorum-sensing (QS) distributed controller that enables scalable collective behavior on low-cost SERB robots powered by ESP32, using only short-range wireless broadcasts and minimal sensing. Each agent converts RSSI into a filtered QS intensity via a single-pole exponential average, infers a surrogate ascent direction with brief yaw micro-scans, and selects among four motion primitives under a hysteretic threemode automaton. Bounded relays, an aging trigger, and density control regulate exploration and overcrowding with constant peragent computation and memory. A four-stage evaluation-bench calibration, open arena, obstacles with constrictions, and scal-ing-demonstrates sub-minute aggregation and bounded load. In the open arena the median time-to-aggregation is 41 s with coverage stability 0.86; with constrictions the success rate is 9% at a median of 72s. With teams up to N = 25 the perrobot packet rate remains ≈ 5.5-5.6 Hz, CPU utilization stays below 35%, and collision rates drop relative to a random-walk baseline. Energy measurements indicate ≈ 2.6 W mean power and 0.21-0.35 Wh per robot over 5-8 min, while injected 20% loss causes only modest shifts (median 52 s in place of 41 s). These results support QS-only, map-free control as a practical path to scalable coordination on resource-constrained platforms.  © 2025 IEEE.
